@prefix thinkr: <http://example.org/thinkr#> .
@prefix owl:   <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs:  <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:  <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dct:   <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix dcmitype: <http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/> .
@prefix xsd:   <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

#############################################
# episodes-tgs.ttl -- canonical, accumulating file, merged 2026-09-12
# from 10 real batch files (episodes-tgs-1-25.ttl through
# episodes-tgs-226-233.ttl), covering TGS interviews #1 through #233.
# All batch files should be moved to archive/ once this merge is
# confirmed correct -- this file is now the single source of truth
# for TGS content going forward, sorted by episode number throughout,
# with tgs:Series.BendNotBreak grouped at the very top per the
# standing convention.
#############################################

<http://example.org/tgs#> a owl:Ontology ;
    owl:imports <http://example.org/thinkr#> ;
    rdfs:comment "Imports tgs-core for Protege compatibility — editing this file alone will resolve thinkr: classes/properties via the catalog file."@en .

tgs:Series.BendNotBreak a thinkr:Series, dcmitype:Collection, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dct:hasPart
        tgs:Interview.TGS_5_BendNotBreakPart1,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_20_MaximumPowerHyperAgents,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_31_SensemakingUncertaintyPurpose,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_42_ModelingDriversMetacrisis,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_50_CriteriaCategoriesResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bend Not Break"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Discovered 2026-07-13 while verifying 3 previously-unrelated Schmachtenberger episode entries — turned out to be a real, distinct 5-part series never before modeled. Confirmed via Nate's own words (a Substack post about Part 3) that the series wasn't originally planned as 5 parts — it grew organically from conversation, first to 4 planned parts, then 5 once Part 3 diverged from its intended topic."@en ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_147_IndyJohar, tgs:Interview.TGS_132_WideBoundaryImpactsOfAI, tgs:Interview.TGS_71_DanielSchmachtenberger, tgs:Interview.TGS_50_CriteriaCategoriesResponse, tgs:Interview.TGS_42_ModelingDriversMetacrisis, tgs:Interview.TGS_31_SensemakingUncertaintyPurpose, tgs:Interview.TGS_20_MaximumPowerHyperAgents, tgs:Interview.TGS_5_BendNotBreakPart1, tgs:Interview.TGS_126_AVisionForBetterment, tgs:Interview.TGS_172_LivingTheChange ;
    .

tgs:Interview.TGS_1_RichardGephardt a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    skos:prefLabel "Richard Gephardt: Democracy, Social Media, and the Case for Public Service"@en ;
    skos:definition "The show's real first-ever episode. Nate hosts former US House Majority/Minority Leader Richard Gephardt — a real, direct professional collaborator on a project called 'advance policy,' not just a first-time guest — for Gephardt's own real first-ever podcast appearance of any kind. Covers the real conflict between social-media business models and functioning democracy, a direct real contrast between the national unity following 9/11 and the division surrounding January 6th, and Gephardt's own real, extensive account of his path from a working-class St. Louis upbringing into public service."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.RichardGephardt ;
    dct:created "2021-12-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/01-dickgephardt> ;
    dct:references tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct date inconsistency in the source material, flagged honestly rather than silently resolved: the show notes list 'Episode 1... Dec 9, 2019' as the issued date, but 'Recorded on: Dec 17, 2021' — an issued date nearly 2 years BEFORE the stated recording date, which is not just unusual but chronologically impossible. This is almost certainly a real site/metadata error, not a genuine fact — further supported by comparison: tgs:Interview.TGS_15_DanielPauly (Episode 15, already built) issued April 2022, only ~2 months after its own Feb 2022 recording — a real, consistent pattern this episode's stated 2019 issue date would badly violate for only 14 episodes' difference. dct:issued deliberately left unasserted here rather than guessing between two implausible options; dct:created (the real recording date) is asserted with confidence. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Tristan Harris and Daniel Schmachtenberger's real joint appearance on the Joe Rogan podcast, directly cited (Schmachtenberger already a real, built Persona in this graph); Tom Downey, directly named as 'a mutual colleague' of Nate and Gephardt, real but not independently confirmed or built in this pass."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A genuinely personal episode from its opening minutes — Nate's first real question isn't about policy but about why an 80-year-old with a storied career isn't simply retired, and Gephardt's own answer is a real, extended account of his working-class St. Louis childhood: a father whose back injury ended his trucking career, a mother who went to work, and a real, specific formative moment — a high school speech teacher personally arranging a Northwestern summer scholarship that Gephardt directly credits as opening his entire adult path. The episode's real analytical core is Gephardt's own direct comparison between the 9/11 aftermath and January 6th: he offers a real, personal account of running through a small Missouri town on the Christmas after 9/11 and finding every house flying an American flag in solidarity with New York, offered as concrete evidence of genuine national unity — directly contrasted with January 6th, which he attributes to Americans who had been, in his own words, effectively 'brainwashed' by an alternative-reality information ecosystem into believing violence was justified. Gephardt is careful throughout to extend real empathy alongside his critique, stating directly that he understands the mindset that produced January 6th even while condemning the outcome. The episode's closing stretch turns genuinely personal and practical: Gephardt's own real daily routine (two meals within an eight-hour window, a standing bowl of raw vegetables) is directly credited by Nate as something he adopted into his own life as a result of this conversation. The episode closes with real, warm testimony to Gephardt's own reputation — Nate directly relays that mutual colleagues refer to him, half-joking, as 'a saint and a national treasure and a superman' — and Gephardt's own closing words return to a real, concrete personal ethic borrowed from his time as a Boy Scout: leave the campsite better than you found it."@en ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_5_BendNotBreakPart1 ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_2_ShannaSwan a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    skos:prefLabel "Shanna Swan: Sperm Counts, Endocrine Disruption, and the Future of Human Reproduction"@en ;
    skos:definition "Swan's real first-ever TGS appearance — one of the podcast's earliest episodes. Nate hosts reproductive epidemiologist Shanna Swan on the real, direct thesis of her then-new 2021 book 'Countdown': endocrine-disrupting chemicals in the environment are driving measurable declines in human sperm count and fertility, with real average sperm concentration falling from roughly 99 to 47 million per milliliter by the 2011 measurement she cites. Covers phthalates, real historical parallels (Tyrone Hayes' atrazine/alligator research at Lake Apopka), and national policy responses — Denmark's fertility campaigns contrasted directly with Israel's real, fully state-funded assisted-reproduction program."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ShannaSwan ;
    dct:created "2021-11-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-01-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/02-shannaswan> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_192_SunilAmrith, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_66_RealityParty ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_23_ThePlasticDetox ;
    skos:scopeNote "Resolves a real, repeatedly-flagged deferred reference: both RR-23 and TGS_99 (Jeremy Grantham) directly cited this real episode without it being built until now — both scopeNotes updated in the same pass. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Tyrone Hayes, directly named for his real atrazine/alligator research, now built (2026-09-02)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A genuinely foundational episode for a real, recurring throughline across this whole graph — this is the real primary source for content directly cited years later in RR-23, RR-15, and TGS_99 (Jeremy Grantham's own direct funding of Swan's research). Swan is careful to preempt an obvious objection early: since conception only requires one sperm, why does a falling average count matter? Her real, direct answer treats sperm count as a distribution, not a threshold — quality and viability requirements mean a large surplus is needed to reliably produce even one successful candidate, and average counts below roughly 40 million per milliliter are clinically considered subfertile. A real, striking comparative policy discussion follows: Denmark's approach, which pairs fertility incentives with real, sustained public attention to environmental exposures, is credited by Swan with actually halting decline in long-term study data — directly contrasted with several Asian countries she names as offering only financial incentives without addressing exposure, which she states plainly 'is not working.' Nate offers a real, speculative extension live in conversation, drawing a direct parallel between a hypothetical future where only a small fraction of fertile men father most children and the real historical case of Genghis Khan's Y-chromosome persisting in a notable percentage of East Asian and, by some estimates, American men today — offered explicitly as speculation, not as something Swan endorses. The episode closes with Swan's own real, direct appeal: she wants this issue to generate the same public 'buzz' climate change has achieved, stating it may be 'perhaps even as serious as that and deeply connected to it' — a real, direct claim about relative severity, not just topical adjacency."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_3_OilBestAndWorstOfFuels a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Oil: It Was the Best of Fuels, It Was the Worst of Fuels"@en ;
    dct:created "2021-12-29"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-01-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ArtBerman ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/03-arthurberman> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_220_AWorldOnThePrecipice, tgs:Interview.TGS_5_BendNotBreakPart1 ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.PeakOil ;
    skos:scopeNote "2026-08-13: real dates confirmed and discusses links added from a complete real transcript + show notes upload (Nate's own first-ever TGS interview, per MJSullivan) — previously a bare stub with no confirmed date. This is Nate and Berman's very first recorded conversation together, explicitly framed by Nate as a primer/introduction to oil basics rather than an advanced deep-dive."@en .

tgs:Interview.TGS_4_ChuckWatson a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Chuck Watson: \"From MAD to NUTS: Risk, Nukes, & Climate Change\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first conversation with his own real, longstanding friend Chuck Watson, whom he'd known for roughly 15 years by this point through Watson's own real hurricane-damage infrastructure maps used on The Oil Drum. Watson directly introduces his own real, coined distinction between MAD (Mutually Assured Destruction, a real, stable deterrence posture) and NUTS (Nuclear Utilization Targeting Strategy, a real, more dangerous doctrine treating nuclear weapons as potentially usable rather than purely deterrent) — arguing the world's drift from the former toward the latter is a genuinely underappreciated risk."@en ;
    dct:created "2021-12-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-01-26"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ChuckWatson ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/04-chuckwatson> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t_MfMSPaLBc> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/TGS04ChuckWatsonTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Watson directly introduces 'risk homeostasis' — a real, established behavioral concept he applies throughout his later appearances too — describing how humans unconsciously recalibrate their own risk tolerance based on recent experience rather than actual underlying danger, making repeated near-misses (rather than actual disasters) a genuine driver of complacency. He's real and direct about a striking real academic estimate: some published risk analyses put the cumulative chance of nuclear war over the preceding 50 years at roughly 60%, framing humanity's continued survival as real, significant luck rather than reliably managed safety. A real, direct historical case anchors his concern about institutional erosion: the Able Archer 83 incident, a real, documented 1983 NATO exercise that nearly triggered an actual Soviet nuclear response due to genuine mutual misperception, offered directly as evidence that even well-intentioned actors can stumble into catastrophe through simple miscalculation. Watson directly connects nuclear risk to this graph's own broader themes: arguing humans are cognitively mismatched for evaluating genuinely low-probability, high-consequence risks, the same underlying mismatch driving climate inaction. Closes on real, direct, practical advice: press political leaders to consider international, not just domestic, consequences of policy choices — using real, current US natural gas exports to Europe as a direct example of a policy serving domestic economic interests while potentially destabilizing international relationships."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, valuable cross-reference surfaced directly in this episode's own real show notes: Dick Gephardt's real, direct first TGS appearance (#1, 'Dick Gephardt on the Iraq War') — distinct from his real, later appearance (#148, already flagged elsewhere in this graph's own tracking as 'Richard Gephardt: Preserving Democracy'). Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic/institutional (MAD, NUTS, hypersonic weapons) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_5_BendNotBreakPart1 a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Daniel Schmachtenberger: \"Bend Not Break Part 1: Energy Blindness\""@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Confirmed 2026-07-13 as Part 1 of a real 5-part series discovered while verifying this episode — not independently known before. Note the URL slug's confirmed typo: 'schmactenberger' not 'schmachtenberger', an inconsistency in TGS's own URLs, not an error introduced here."@en ;
    skos:definition "The first part of the Bend Not Break series and the first recorded Hagens-Schmachtenberger conversation, in which Daniel 'flips the script' and interviews Nate to lay down the biophysical foundation the later parts build on. Nate gives his elevator pitch: energy is the currency of life; agriculture and then fossil hydrocarbons, drawn down 10 million times faster than they were sequestered, gave humanity surplus that economics treats as interest rather than principal and prices only at the cost of extraction (tgs:Concept.CostOfExtractionPricing). Americans live on vast exosomatic energy (tgs:Concept.ExosomaticEnergy) -- the equivalent of 500 billion fossil workers (tgs:Concept.EnergySlaves). Global GDP and energy stay tightly coupled, and national decoupling is mostly offshoring. Efficiency feeds growth through the Jevons paradox, because most technology creates new uses of energy (tgs:Concept.TwoCategoriesOfTechnology), unless its gains are diverted outside the superorganism (tgs:Concept.JevonsDividend). Renewables are rebuildables with lower full-system EROI (tgs:Concept.Rebuildables). The responses Nate offers are advance policy (tgs:Concept.AdvancePolicy), repricing non-renewable inputs while untaxing labor (tgs:Concept.Untax), and a scout team that simplifies first. Daniel frames bend versus break and the coordination problem between nations, and closes on holding outrage and the beauty of life together."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-01-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-01-26"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/05-daniel-schmactenberger> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/TGS05DanielSchmachtenbergerTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.BendNotBreak ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 1 ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.ExosomaticEnergy, tgs:Concept.CostOfExtractionPricing, tgs:Concept.TwoCategoriesOfTechnology, tgs:Concept.JevonsDividend,
        tgs:Concept.AdvancePolicy, tgs:Concept.Untax, tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness, tgs:Concept.EnergySlaves, tgs:Concept.Superorganism,
        tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple, tgs:Concept.KleibersLaw, tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox, tgs:Concept.EnergyReturnOnInvestment,
        tgs:Concept.Rebuildables, tgs:Concept.EmbeddedGrowthObligation, tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification,
        tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure, tgs:Concept.DunbarsNumber, tgs:Concept.TheoryOfChange,
        tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis ;
    dct:references tgs:Series.BendNotBreak, tgs:Interview.TGS_1_RichardGephardt, tgs:Interview.TGS_3_OilBestAndWorstOfFuels,
        tgs:Work.EconomicsForTheFutureBeyondTheSuperorganism, tgs:Work.RealityBlind ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "INTRO: Nate met Daniel about two years earlier, through friends who urged them to talk; their first phone call ran three hours and Daniel 'connected ideas that were in my head that I had never connected.' The intent was to merge Nate's macro superorganism, tethered to energy and carbon, with Daniel's AI-driven, attention-capturing 'micro-organism'; instead Daniel interviewed Nate. Daniel describes his own path from activism to systems science, catastrophic-risk forecasting and the Consilience Project: framing the metacrisis and the design criteria of its solution so that 'a lot more of the world can start innovating in a well-informed direction.' FRAME: Nate introduces Marvin Harris's cultural materialism -- superstructure, structure, infrastructure -- which they both use, and says all three must move together (tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure). ELEVATOR PITCH: energy is the currency of life; 290,000 of our 300,000 years were spent as hunter-gatherers working about 20 hours a week; agriculture in at least seven places after the climate stabilized gave storable surplus, population, trade and specialists; since the 1800s we draw down fossil carbon 10 million times faster than it was sequestered and treat it as interest rather than principal (tgs:Concept.CostOfExtractionPricing). A barrel of oil (5.7 million BTU, about 1,700 kWh) does about five years of human labor, yet we pay only the cost of extraction. There is no substitute for energy, so GDP cannot yet decouple from it; the growth-dependent system kicks the can with debt while central banks expand their balance sheets; a superorganism imposes downward causation on its cells (tgs:Concept.Superorganism); most people think technology drives progress when it is technology plus energy (tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness). THERMODYNAMICS: Daniel calls life 'an entropy pump.' Nate explains the maximum power principle to Daniel (forest thermal transects; Kleiber's law; global GDP scaling) (tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple, tgs:Concept.KleibersLaw). Daniel: grain and oil are both stored sunlight accessed by technology -- Nate's current, old and ancient sunlight. Storable grain meant surviving famine, growing population, warfare, defense, private property and inheritance -- 'our species ate the apple'; Daniel calls it the beginning of the Anthropocene; Nate notes consumption inequality began with stored surplus; civilization grew beyond Dunbar's number (tgs:Concept.DunbarsNumber). Nate stresses human and cultural plasticity: we are living an anomaly of exosomatic energy (tgs:Concept.ExosomaticEnergy) -- about 2,500 kcal a day inside the body versus over 200,000 outside it; 100 billion barrels of oil equivalent a year, or 500 billion human-labor equivalents behind 5 billion workers (tgs:Concept.EnergySlaves); economics credits capital and labor instead. Daniel traces the pattern back to fire and stone tools: hunting capacity grew faster than prey could evolve. FOOD AND POPULATION: half a billion people before oil, eight billion now; humans and livestock are 98% of mammal biomass; Haber-Bosch fertilizer means 60% of the nitrogen in our bodies has a natural-gas signature; the food system now spends 10-14 calories per food calorie in full cycle. COUPLING: GDP and energy grew about one for one before 1970 and at about 99% since; the US and UK decouple by importing (57 plus 17 barrels of oil equivalent per American); recent apparent decoupling is a financial-market artifact. Daniel: national metrics such as the Gini coefficient also externalize; Nate: humanity needs wide-boundary thinking. JEVONS: Nate explains the paradox and the two categories of technology, and tech's deflation against depletion's inflation (tgs:Concept.TwoCategoriesOfTechnology, tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox); Daniel adds the supply side (new market sectors) and niche-filling; Nate proposes a 'Jevons paradox dividend' (tgs:Concept.JevonsDividend). Daniel: so we need a post-growth economy, since the embedded growth obligation of interest meets diminishing returns on hydrocarbons (tgs:Concept.EmbeddedGrowthObligation); Nate: central banks paper over it until the Great Simplification (tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification). RENEWABLES: oil is like hemoglobin -- liquid, dense, a 'meal already created'; renewables are rebuildables (tgs:Concept.Rebuildables); 2019's growth in electricity demand exceeded all solar capacity ever built; fossil share steady near 83%; we burn more wood than 150 years ago. Daniel names three limits (EROI, energy quality, non-energy uses); Nate gives oil's historical 100:1 EROI, optimistic 12:1 for solar and wind against 8:1 for coal, but about 4:1 full-system after intermittency, perhaps 5-6:1 with overbuilding (tgs:Concept.EnergyReturnOnInvestment). Electricity is about 20% of energy use; the human economy is about 1,000 times larger than in 1500. EXTERNALITIES: we underpay for the main input and do not pay for pollution; 'turning billions of barrels of ancient sunlight into microliters of dopamine'; Tim Garrett's GDP-CO2 tracking; no industry would be profitable with full externalities (coal 18-20 cents per kWh versus 4). We will not voluntarily degrow; we will shrink when the papering-over fails. Bend gives a saner pathway; break leads into existential risks. Repricing non-renewable inputs while untaxing labor (tgs:Concept.Untax); materials track GDP one for one (two pounds of non-renewable materials per dollar; 3.1 million pounds per American lifetime); gasoline is only 40% of a barrel, so electric cars alone do not end oil demand. Daniel: mining tailings dwarf municipal waste; we need a closed-loop economy on renewable energy, which requires a new economic system; every high school should teach what it takes to make a phone; TSMC and Taiwan. Nate: regionalize supply chains for essentials; 9% of materials are recycled; with Dick Gephardt he is working on advance policy (tgs:Concept.AdvancePolicy; tgs:Interview.TGS_1_RichardGephardt). Daniel: the market is subsidized by nature from a savings account about to run out. BEND VERSUS BREAK: bend means a smaller economy aimed at wellbeing and social capital; break means it happens unplanned, through supply-chain collapse or war; Daniel adds currency collapse. Asked how to bend when whoever bends first loses (tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap), Nate answers top-down plans plus bottom-up awakening: a 'scout team' that simplifies first and beats the rush (tgs:Concept.TheoryOfChange). Daniel says culture, social structures and technology must evolve together, 'a whole ecosystem of solutions.' COPING: Daniel recalls Krishnamurti urging audiences to take the topics seriously -- 'the Death Star is about to take the planet out' -- rejects both suicide and hedonism as answers to nihilism ('I can die failing, trying'), says the path 'does end up looking like threading a needle,' and holds two sayings together: 'if you aren't outraged, you aren't paying attention' and, from Gibran and Hafez, 'if you aren't overwhelmed by the beauty of life, you aren't paying attention.' Nate: Americans use 100 times the energy they need, Europeans 50; friends, dogs and nature keep him going."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-18 from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the modeled-only entry (the existing rdfs:comment is kept). The prefLabel now carries the guest name, matching the site title. The show notes record no YouTube link. The site description calls this the first of a five-part series; Nate's intro anticipates three. Spoken figures recorded but not checked: Nate says a day of manual work generates 'six tenths of a watt' of power (likely a slip for a much larger figure; the show notes cite section 4.3 of Nate's paper for the five-years-per-barrel claim); he says global GDP scales to 'the two-thirds power' while animal metabolism scales to three-quarters; the coal full-cost figure is linked to a 2011 Annals of the New York Academy of Sciences paper; the GDP-energy '99%' figure is linked to a synapse9 draft. Transcription artifacts: 'insucient' is insufficient; 'dicult' is difficult; 'silicone wafers' is silicon. Mentioned but not linked: Arthur Berman (the show-note 'energy depletion' link is TGS_3); Dick Gephardt (TGS_1, advance policy). Mentioned and already in the graph: Marvin Harris (tgs:Persona.MarvinHarris, credited on tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure) and William Stanley Jevons (tgs:Persona.WilliamStanleyJevons). Mentioned but not built: Tim Garrett, Imperial College London (Untax research), the Untax project (untax.org), TSMC, J. Krishnamurti, Kahlil Gibran, Hafez, the Reality 101 energy video playlist. Daniel's first use of 'threading a needle' here anticipates tgs:Concept.ThirdAttractor, which he names in TGS_42. Candidate concepts named but NOT minted: life as an 'entropy pump' (close to tgs:Concept.LocalizedSyntropySystemicEntropy, TGS_42); current, old and ancient sunlight; holding outrage and beauty together (close to tgs:Concept.PostTragic, TGS_31); a supply-chain curriculum (what it takes to make a phone)."@en ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_71_DanielSchmachtenberger, tgs:Interview.TGS_50_CriteriaCategoriesResponse, tgs:Interview.TGS_42_ModelingDriversMetacrisis, tgs:Interview.TGS_31_SensemakingUncertaintyPurpose, tgs:Interview.TGS_20_MaximumPowerHyperAgents, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_55_7KeyInterventionsForTheFuture ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Hello, today's conversation is with my friend and colleague Daniel Schmachtenberger. Daniel and I met a couple years ago. Turns out he's working on what he refers to as the metacrisis, how climate and geopolitics and artificial intelligence and everything fit together into this collective of risks. I had never heard of him and he had never heard of me or my work or my network looking at energy, money, growth, ecology. And we talked on the phone and the first time I talked to him, I'll never forget it, we talked for three hours. And at one time in the conversation I got goosebumps on my left arm because he connected ideas that were in my head that I had never connected. And those of you that know Daniel and listen to his podcast know that he often does someone's thinking for them. He's that sort of an intellect. Daniel's current main focus is on the risks that social media and artificial intelligence pose as this exponentially growing function that's hijacking our minds as individuals, and at a cultural level is impeding or even negating our collective ability to orient and adapt to our current challenges. Daniel and I always talk for hours. I wish I would've recorded our previous long phone conversations, but we decided to start recording with this one. Our intent was to lay the foundation of what I refer to as the energy blindness of our culture and how many people miss the relationship between technology, energy, money, and growth, and what that means for our future constraints. What we intended on doing is merging my story on the macro superorganism that we self-organize towards profits. The profits are tethered to energy, which are tethered to carbon, and there's a growth imperative in there. And merge that with his story of an AI induced profit seeking, call it micro-organism, capturing our attention into a grand synthesis of the human predicament and how we can steer society in the future towards bending instead of breaking in the coming decade. In an unexpected way, Daniel ended up interviewing me in this podcast, so it was a bit weird. But I hope you find it helpful and interesting background to the part two in the series in the near future, which is going to be taking a deeper dive of how these things fit together. And part three will be the bend versus break, how we should think about this, what we should do as individuals and as a culture. I hope you enjoy the conversation."@en ;
    .

tgs:Interview.TGS_6_HermanDaly a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Herman Daly: \"Toward an Ecological Economics\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real conversation with Herman Daly — a founder of ecological economics, former World Bank senior economist — recorded roughly 20 years after reading Daly's Valuing the Earth directly caused Nate to leave his Wall Street career. Opens with Nate's own real, extraordinary personal disclosure: he once called and emailed Daly asking to become his PhD student; Daly, unable to take students at the time due to being 'on the outs' with his department at LSU, personally connected Nate instead to his colleague Josh Farley (already a real, built Persona in this graph) — the actual, direct reason Nate earned his PhD at the University of Vermont. Daly traces his own real intellectual path through Nicholas Georgescu-Roegen at Vanderbilt (whose entropy-law economics Daly encountered as a student, in progress toward what became Georgescu-Roegen's own foundational book) and Rachel Carson's Silent Spring."@en ;
    dct:created "2021-10-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-02-02"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.HermanDaly ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/06-herman-daly> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_185_MythsShapingOurEconomies ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8LCTa4kEPl0> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/TGS06HermanDalyTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.ForTheCommonGood, tgs:Work.ValuingTheEarth, tgs:Interview.TGS_27_JoeTainter ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, direct rebuttal of neoclassical economics' foundational assumptions structures the conversation's intellectual core: Daly names the Cobb-Douglas production function and Robert Solow's own real work directly, arguing their treatment of capital and labor as substitutable for energy and materials — with energy's real contribution to output mathematically tiny in standard models — fundamentally misrepresents a biophysical reality where energy 'cannot be substituted.' Daly is direct and unsparing about the real consequence: economics 'underpays for the main input to our economies' by treating energy's actual value (a barrel of oil doing roughly five years of human labor) as merely its extraction cost. A real, pointed philosophical distinction closes the conversation's advice to young people: Daly names philosophical materialism — not consumerism, but the deeper claim that 'the world consists only of matter and motion' — as a real, underappreciated danger, arguing it leads directly to determinism, which in turn undercuts any belief that policy or individual choice can matter at all. Real, direct personal warmth closes the episode without sentimentality: Daly's own final words are 'I'm just very glad that you're devoting your youthful energy to these things... God bless you and your work,' a genuine blessing from mentor to former student, not a scripted TV sign-off."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Nicholas Georgescu-Roegen (Daly's own real PhD advisor, directly credited as the origin of Daly's entire intellectual shift — a real, second-order mentorship lineage: Georgescu-Roegen shaped Daly, Daly shaped Nate); Kenneth Boulding (real, directly cited economist); Reiner Kümmel (independently reconfirmed here — already known from the Herman Daly citation chain via Frankly_23's own Mordor Economy build and TGS_84's Jancovici build)."@en ;
    .


