tgs:Interview.TGS_17_ChuckWatson a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Chuck Watson: \"Nuclear War – All the Questions You Were Afraid to Ask\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct third conversation with Chuck Watson, prompted specifically by real listener requests for a deeper technical dive on nuclear risk. Nate directly introduces Watson as someone who 'never calls attention to himself,' and discloses learning real, new things about him even after 15 years of friendship — framing Watson's willingness to speak publicly on this topic as a deliberate, considered choice given his own real, natural reticence."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-04-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-05-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ChuckWatson ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/17-chuck-watson-nuclear-war> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_159_TheRealWork ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hvXO_rHbS9c> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/05/TGS17ChuckWatsonTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_4_ChuckWatson, tgs:Interview.TGS_214_TristanHarris ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Watson directly walks through the real technical distinction between fission and fusion weapons, and real, specific concepts like 'dial-a-yield' (modern weapons whose explosive yield can be adjusted before deployment) — arguing this real, increased flexibility paradoxically makes nuclear weapons feel more usable, not less dangerous. A real, direct extension of Nate's own established 'supernormal stimuli' teaching concept (now formalized as a Concept in this graph) emerges organically mid-conversation: Nate proposes nuclear weapons themselves, not just money, as the largest real supernormal stimulus humans have created, given their real, many-orders-of-magnitude destructive scale relative to any ancestral weapon. A real, genuinely vulnerable personal disclosure closes the conversation: Watson admits that professionals in his own field must partially 'shut off' their humanity to think clearly about targeting and blast calculations, describing this as real, personally frightening — crediting a real, direct visit to Beirut and witnessing the aftermath of a comparatively 'trivial' conventional conflict with helping restore his own perspective on nuclear war's real, vastly greater stakes. Watson's own real, direct definition of patriotism closes the episode, quoting 19th-century senator Carl Schurz directly: genuine patriotism means ensuring one's own country does what's right, not reflexive loyalty regardless of conduct."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Tristan Harris's real first TGS appearance (already directly cross-referenced in this episode's own show notes) remains unbuilt — only his second appearance, TGS_214, exists in this graph; this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic/institutional (MIRV, Proud Prophet, Intermediate Nuclear Forces Treaty) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_18_ThomasMurphy a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Thomas Murphy: \"Physics and Planetary Ambitions\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real conversation with his own real friend Thomas Murphy, physics professor at UC San Diego and author of the real, long-running 'Do the Math' blog, working through why continued energy growth is a physical, not merely political or economic, impossibility. Murphy's own real, striking calculation: at current growth rates, humanity's total energy consumption would exceed the entire galaxy's energy output within just a few thousand years — a real, direct demonstration that exponential growth in a finite physical system cannot continue indefinitely, regardless of the energy source."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-03-15"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ThomasMurphy ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/18-tom-murphy> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_159_TheRealWork ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KXertTq0knU> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/05/TGS18ThomasMurphyTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.EnergyAndHumanAmbitions, tgs:Interview.TGS_6_HermanDaly ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Murphy directly grounds his argument in basic thermodynamics: physical growth of any kind, including energy use, is fundamentally bound by the laws governing energy and matter, meaning economic 'decoupling' from physical throughput is a real, direct violation of physics if pursued indefinitely rather than a genuine escape route. A real, striking waste-heat calculation closes his energy-limits argument: continued 2-3% annual energy growth would, within a surprisingly short historical timeframe, generate enough waste heat alone to boil the oceans, independent of any greenhouse gas effect whatsoever — offered as a real, direct proof that energy growth itself, not just its byproducts, has hard physical limits. He's real and direct about a psychological mechanism underlying collective inaction: humans are, in his own real phrase, 'programmed to ignore' slow-moving, abstract threats, a real cognitive tendency he connects directly to the discount rate humans intuitively apply to distant future harm. Murphy is candid and unusually direct about his own emotional relationship to collapse risk: having already moved through real stages of grief regarding civilization's trajectory, he states plainly that earlier collapse would leave more real resources available for recovery than continued 'successful' delay — arguing continued short-term success at avoiding collapse is actually the most dangerous possible outcome, since it only compounds the eventual overshoot. Closes on real, considered hope distinct from naive optimism: while worried younger generations understand growth's limits abstractly but still direct their concern outward ('the billionaire's problem, not their own habits'), Murphy expresses real, genuine longer-term hope in human adaptability — arguing someone born into a post-collapse world would simply experience it as normal, and could, in a real sense, do just fine."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is largely encyclopedic/institutional (thermodynamics, exponential growth, Myers-Briggs) rather than pointing to further individuals — Herman Daly is directly cross-referenced and already built, not a new find; Thomas Malthus (real, directly cited, historical figure, not built)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_19_SimonMichaux a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Simon Michaux: \"Minerals Blindness\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with geometallurgist Simon Michaux (Geological Survey of Finland), introducing his own real, direct parallel to this graph's own core concept of energy blindness: 'minerals blindness,' the culture-wide failure to recognize and plan around real, finite mineral and material limits the way energy limits are increasingly discussed. Real, striking figures ground the argument directly: base metals are recycled at only 30-60%, technology metals essentially not at all, and nuclear power — even scaled aggressively — cannot physically ramp up fast enough to replace fossil fuels within any realistic near-term timeline, given the current real pace of roughly one to two new plants built globally per year."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-03-29"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-05-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.SimonMichaux ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/19-simon-michaux> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_160_AStateOfTheWorldAddress ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O0pt3ioQuNc> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/05/TGS19SimonMichauxTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Michaux's own real, direct framing of renewables as 'the right answer to the wrong question' anchors the episode's central argument: the green-transition conversation focuses almost entirely on energy supply while largely ignoring the real, physical mineral inputs that transition itself requires — copper extraction alone, he notes, is set to become measurably more difficult even as demand for it accelerates. A real, concrete regional case study closes the conversation's practical section: Cuba's real, documented response to the 1990s Soviet oil embargo (widespread urban agriculture, radically reduced consumption, decentralized local production) is offered directly as a real, lived precedent for what societal adaptation to sudden resource constraint can actually look like, rather than a purely theoretical scenario. Real, direct personal warmth closes the episode: after roughly 75 minutes of dense technical material, Nate and Michaux close with mutual, genuine Australian-inflected banter ('You too, mate' / 'Nate, you're a mate as well'), and Nate's own real, stated hope to visit Michaux in person in Finland that summer — a plan later confirmed as actually fulfilled, per Michaux's own real reference to it in his second appearance (TGS_49)."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Paul Ehrlich (real TGS episode #9, directly cross-referenced — already a known, deferred citation elsewhere in this graph); the real Cuban post-Soviet oil-embargo response, treated here as a real historical case study rather than a citable individual."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_20_MaximumPowerHyperAgents a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Daniel Schmachtenberger: \"Bend Not Break Part 2: Maximum Power and Hyper Agents\""@en ;
    skos:definition "The second part of the Bend Not Break series and the game-theoretic core of the Hagens-Schmachtenberger dialogue. Nate's metabolic account -- a superorganism optimizing financial surplus tethered to energy -- meets Daniel's account of the dynamics that generate catastrophic risk. Daniel summarizes Nate's work as an exponential embedded growth obligation on finance bound to a finite world (tgs:Concept.EmbeddedGrowthObligation), with efficiency gains rebounding into growth for every widely used input, not just energy (tgs:Concept.GeneralizedJevonsParadox). He tells the story of the plow ending animism: advantageous technology is obligate and codes behavior, values and culture (tgs:Concept.TechnologyIsNotValuesNeutral), so ethics must be designed into it (tgs:Concept.AxiologicalDesign). He then constructs the multipolar trap -- tragedy of the commons, arms race and market race -- and the asymmetry favoring opportunity over risk (tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap, tgs:Concept.OpportunityRiskAsymmetry). He restates the maximum power principle for markets and agents (tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple) and introduces hyper-agents, who maximize returns on agency and set the incentive landscape for everyone else (tgs:Concept.HyperAgents), with money as the unit of maximum optionality (tgs:Concept.MoneyAsMaximumOptionality). He frames the economy as atoms, energy and bits (tgs:Concept.AtomsEnergyBits). Nate offers 'the last can to kick' -- meaning with fewer resources (tgs:Concept.TheLastCanToKick). Daniel closes on head, heart and will (tgs:Concept.HeartWillMind) and on understanding the system turning problems 'from impossible to hard' (tgs:Concept.ImpossibleToHard)."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-05-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-05-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/20-daniel-schmactenberger> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/05/TGS20DanielSchmactenbergerPart2Transcript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Nkv5mpBA8o4> ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.BendNotBreak ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 2 ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.HyperAgents, tgs:Concept.MoneyAsMaximumOptionality, tgs:Concept.GeneralizedJevonsParadox, tgs:Concept.AtomsEnergyBits,
        tgs:Concept.AxiologicalDesign, tgs:Concept.TheLastCanToKick, tgs:Concept.ImpossibleToHard, tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap,
        tgs:Concept.OpportunityRiskAsymmetry, tgs:Concept.TechnologyIsNotValuesNeutral, tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple,
        tgs:Concept.MaximizingReturnsOnPower, tgs:Concept.EmbeddedGrowthObligation, tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox,
        tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure, tgs:Concept.HeartWillMind, tgs:Concept.ThreeByThreeResponseGrid,
        tgs:Concept.KleibersLaw, tgs:Concept.DunbarsNumber, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.TragedyOfTheCommons ;
    dct:references tgs:Series.BendNotBreak, tgs:Interview.TGS_5_BendNotBreakPart1, tgs:Series.GreatSimplificationAnimatedSeries,
        tgs:Work.EconomicsForTheFutureBeyondTheSuperorganism, tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Work.TechnologyIsNotValuesNeutral ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "RECAP: Nate summarizes Part 1 (tgs:Interview.TGS_5_BendNotBreakPart1): energy as the currency of life; about 100 billion barrels of oil equivalent of fossil sunlight a year; renewables are viable, but whether they can power a 19-terawatt system as it is now is a big question; Jevons paradox; oil depletion. Daniel praises Nate's book (tgs:Work.RealityBlind) and frames their shared insight: the many catastrophic risks -- resource wars, supply chains, planetary boundaries, AI, bio-risk -- cannot be solved separately, like treating heart disease while the patient ages; one must address the generative dynamics, as anti-aging medicine addresses pathogenesis. NATE'S SUPERORGANISM: we self-organize to optimize financial representations of surplus, tethered to energy and carbon; no politician or billionaire steers it; planning is outsourced to financial markets (tgs:Work.EconomicsForTheFutureBeyondTheSuperorganism). DANIEL'S MINIMAL SUMMARY: finance has an exponential embedded growth obligation (tgs:Concept.EmbeddedGrowthObligation) bound to a finite world; Jevons paradox plus the money-energy coupling (the 'Garrett relation') means capital seeks any positive return and efficiency only widens the surface of profitability; the cloud has a physical substrate (Nate: about 18% of electricity); renewables' EROI means more oil to build them. MONEY: Nate explains that commercial banks create about 95% of money as loans, with no biophysical reference, and that interest is not created with the principal; world debt doubles every eight to nine years and GDP every 25; at 3% growth, energy and material use would double in 25-30 years (material footprint up 2.8% a year since 1900). Daniel: country metrics on global supply chains are 'greenwashing gibberish' -- a good Gini coefficient or joules-to-dollars ratio can rest on imported slave labor; Nate: 57 barrels of oil equivalent per American plus 17 embodied in imports. CLIMATE: India's coal use rises to power air conditioning in the heat; an Indian contact's 'genocide of negligence' framing of colonial extraction; wet-bulb heat and resource wars can escalate long before 'Venus-ification.' Nate: climate change is the largest risk by two orders of magnitude but not in the top ten for the next ten years; 93% of the heat has gone into the oceans. Aza Raskin's Earth Species Project; Nate fears people would shrug, and calls for 'a new sacred.' THE PLOW (tgs:Concept.TechnologyIsNotValuesNeutral): Daniel recalls the three layers (Marvin Harris; tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure) and says tech is 'values affective,' citing the forthcoming Consilience paper (tgs:Work.TechnologyIsNotValuesNeutral). Following Ken Wilber, animism died after the ox-drawn plow: a hunter can honor the buffalo's spirit, but one cannot castrate, yoke and beat an ox all day and stay animist. Principle one: highly advantageous tech is obligate. Principle two: it codes behavior, hence values, hence culture. Historians also link the plow to patriarchy and male gods, surplus, private property, empires beyond Dunbar's number (tgs:Concept.DunbarsNumber), class and formal marriage (David Graeber adds nuance); the printing press to the Reformation and democracy. Hence 'axiological design' (tgs:Concept.AxiologicalDesign). Nate is moved, and reframes his question: is surplus values-agnostic? Daniel: fire, agriculture, oil and digital tech were successive spikes in energy extraction; Nate: those were the cans we kicked, and the only can left is in our minds (tgs:Concept.TheLastCanToKick). There was a Jevons paradox in agriculture too, and a superorganism dynamic. MAXIMUM POWER: Nate defines the maximum power principle and Kleiber's law (tgs:Concept.KleibersLaw); Americans use about 100 times their endosomatic energy exosomatically; Lotka. Daniel: whoever exploits an energy source, and the memes justifying it, gets selected; humans do niche-filling 'on crack.' MULTIPOLAR TRAP (tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap): the restrained buffalo hunters do not save the buffalo -- 'I have the ability to kill it, but not the ability to protect it, therefore I actually should race.' Three cases: the tragedy of the commons (cutting trees; if the US taxes carbon and China does not, it cedes the century), the arms race (nukes and AI weapons, where agreements cannot be verified), and the market race to first-mover advantage, which favors opportunity over risk (tgs:Concept.OpportunityRiskAsymmetry) -- also because benefits are first-order and measurable while harms are nth-order and unknown. Nate: insect biomass falls 1-2% a year and we cannot even study it; Daniel: we have the resources, but the trap does not orient them. Daniel restates the maximum power principle for markets: any positive return, energy or capital, will be exploited (tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple). HYPER-AGENTS (tgs:Concept.HyperAgents): a Consilience paper in progress; a tiny power-law tail of people (Genghis Khan, Alexander) who maximize returns on agency and create the incentive landscapes others live in (tgs:Concept.MaximizingReturnsOnPower); agency is not personal wealth (Kissinger, Xi Jinping). Money is a unit of agency and optionality; the global reserve currency maximizes stability and optionality; the fastest OODA loop wins (tgs:Concept.MoneyAsMaximumOptionality). Nate: is the superorganism driving the hyper-agents or the reverse? Daniel: causation is recursive. Money began as extrinsic motivation for work no one wanted to do; Einstein and Jesus were intrinsically motivated, while Eisenhower and Constantine converted their work into game-theoretic power. Nate: we need wider-boundary hyper-agents, but the system does not reward them. HEAD, HEART, WILL (tgs:Concept.HeartWillMind): as a child at Greenpeace and PETA protests, Daniel saw caring activists who were sure to lose; any two of the three without the third 'and the world dies'; narrow values with will and mind make the hyper-agent; all three with wide values are 'a minimum necessary requirement.' Should we widen hyper-agents' values or empower people with wide values? Both, and change how society develops people at scale. NEXT TIME: Nate proposes triage, transition and long-term horizontally and global, national, community and individual vertically (tgs:Concept.ThreeByThreeResponseGrid). AGENCY: understanding the superorganism can feel disempowering; Daniel says seeing shared underlying dynamics moves problems 'from impossible to hard' and can cure nihilism; delayed gratification, staying with problems, treating failure as learning; a project on hard questions as a better orienting frame than the Sustainable Development Goals (tgs:Concept.ImpossibleToHard). ATOMS, ENERGY, BITS (tgs:Concept.AtomsEnergyBits): Peter Thiel's 'atoms and bits,' Metcalfe's law and software's scaling; Forrest Landry adds energy. Atoms are finite and must be closed-loop; energy has a fixed incoming bandwidth; bits are limited by atoms, energy and human attention; a generalized Jevons paradox applies to compute, steel and any widely used input (tgs:Concept.GeneralizedJevonsParadox), so efficiency needs a binding system, not incentives. Nate: the reverse is starting -- as energy gets dearer, everything does, including solar panels. Daniel: prices are the cost of extraction plus margin, not of making the substrate or its externalities; 'stealing from the balance sheet of nature' counts as industriousness, and a sustainable market would make almost nothing profitable. Nate: synthetic methane from overbuilt renewables at about $10 a gallon could support a society, 'but not this society'; perhaps 5-12 terawatts rather than 19, as the 'fossil armies' retire."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-18 from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the modeled-only entry. The prefLabel now carries the guest name, matching the site title 'Bend not Break #2'; the existing YouTube replay link is kept (the show notes record none). Nate's intro and the site description call this a three- or four-part series; it grew to five. Spoken claims recorded but not checked: Nate says 8% of men alive carry Genghis Khan's Y chromosome, and Daniel '8 or 11 or whatever' -- the show-note link says 1 in 200 (0.5%); 'about 18%' of electricity for the cloud and devices is linked to a general article; 95% of money from bank lending; world debt doubling every eight to nine years against GDP every 25; material footprint up 2.8% a year since 1900; the Indian contact's '$50 trillion' colonial extraction figure; Nate's 'Kleiber's law... to the two-thirds power' (the graph's tgs:Concept.KleibersLaw records the conventional three-quarters). The Bank of England paper on money creation is mentioned but not linked in the show notes. Transcription artifacts: 'Alfred Latka' is Alfred Lotka; 'Cal Park' is unclear in the source; 'sufficently' is sufficiently; 'als' is also. Mentioned but not linked: Aza Raskin (tgs:Persona.AzaRaskin) and tgs:Organization.EarthSpeciesProject; Steve Keen (tgs:Persona.SteveKeen: 'technology without energy is a sculpture'); David Graeber (tgs:Persona.DavidGraeber, his 'new book' unnamed). Mentioned but not built: Ken Wilber (Sex, Ecology, Spirit), Louis Mumford (that is, Lewis Mumford), Peter Thiel, Forrest Landry, Henry Kissinger, Xi Jinping, Constantine, Eisenhower, the Council of Nicaea, the Sustainable Development Goals. Candidate concepts named but NOT minted: 'genocide of negligence'; tech as 'values affective' rather than neutral, positive or negative (inside tgs:Concept.TechnologyIsNotValuesNeutral); stealing from nature's balance sheet (close to tgs:Concept.GameTheoreticVersusRealValue, TGS_42); the recursive top-down and bottom-up causation between hyper-agents and the superorganism (inside tgs:Concept.HyperAgents)."@en ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_202_SamanthaSweetwater, tgs:Interview.TGS_71_DanielSchmachtenberger, tgs:Interview.TGS_50_CriteriaCategoriesResponse, tgs:Interview.TGS_42_ModelingDriversMetacrisis, tgs:Interview.TGS_31_SensemakingUncertaintyPurpose ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "This week's podcast is a second installment of a conversation with my friend Daniel Schmachtenberger. We intended this series to be a three-part series on bend versus break, but we got a little sidetracked with both interesting and, I hope, relevant and helpful tangents on energy, maximum power, animist religion, and the importance and relevance of hyper-agents or those human individuals who have outsized influence on our cultural direction. Daniel and I come to this with wildly different backgrounds, and we've kind of found each other on the internet and are learning from each other, exploring, and trying to figure out the path ahead and basically share our revelations and our thought process publicly because I think we need a lot more people engaged in these questions in this space at this time, I hope you enjoy this conversation. I certainly did. Here is part two with Daniel Schmachtenberger."@en ;
    .

