    skos:prefLabel "Daniel Schmachtenberger: \"Bend Not Break Part 3: Sensemaking, Uncertainty, and Purpose\""@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Nate's own Substack post about this episode explicitly says the conversation diverged from its planned topic, 'and this divergence means we've extended this Bend Not Break series to (at least) 5 installments' — this is the episode where the series grew from its original shorter plan."@en ;
    skos:definition "The third part of the Bend Not Break series, which Nate planned as a comparison of their two systems overviews but which became, as Daniel later calls it, a 'psychological interlude': how to take in the metacrisis in a way that gives agency rather than despair. It is the inner, personal side of Daniel's work. Modern economies offer rewards stripped of the work and deeper nourishment they evolved with -- fast food, porn, social media and quick certainty (tgs:Concept.OneMarshmallowRewardCircuits) -- and false certainty, catastrophic as much as naive, can justify terrible acts (tgs:Concept.PrematureCertainty). An open society needs everyone to make sense of the world well (tgs:Concept.SensemakingPrerequisiteOfOpenSociety). Following David Bohm, the deepest generator function is perceiving the world as separable parts (tgs:Concept.FragmentedConsciousness). Daniel tells how, at 15, a false certainty of inevitable extinction gave way to Buckminster Fuller's eggshell image of a discrete phase shift (tgs:Concept.DiscretePhaseShift). He describes Marc Gafni's post-tragic stance: service to the sacredness of life without needing certainty of success (tgs:Concept.PostTragic). The episode closes on heart, will and mind working together (tgs:Concept.HeartWillMind)."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-07-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-08-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/31-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/TGS31DanielSchmactenbergerPART03Transcript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kTFqnPEyweE> ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.BendNotBreak ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 3 ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.OneMarshmallowRewardCircuits, tgs:Concept.PrematureCertainty, tgs:Concept.SensemakingPrerequisiteOfOpenSociety,
        tgs:Concept.FragmentedConsciousness, tgs:Concept.DiscretePhaseShift, tgs:Concept.PostTragic, tgs:Concept.HeartWillMind,
        tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli, tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox, tgs:Concept.EmbeddedGrowthObligation,
        tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis ;
    dct:references tgs:Series.BendNotBreak, tgs:Interview.TGS_5_BendNotBreakPart1, tgs:Interview.TGS_20_MaximumPowerHyperAgents,
        tgs:Series.GreatSimplificationAnimatedSeries, tgs:Work.EconomicsForTheFutureBeyondTheSuperorganism, tgs:Work.RealityBlind,
        tgs:Work.WholenessAndTheImplicateOrder, tgs:Work.DietForANewAmerica ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "PLAN: Nate proposes to give his elevator pitch, have Daniel lay out his metanarrative of the metacrisis and compare them, ask why grand narratives provoke pushback, and set up Part 4 on criteria for civilization. NATE'S PITCH: humans are a social species that has self-organized into an economy optimizing financial surplus tethered to energy, mostly fossil, drawn down 10 million times faster than it was sequestered, with waste left out of prices (tgs:Work.EconomicsForTheFutureBeyondTheSuperorganism; tgs:Series.GreatSimplificationAnimatedSeries); climate and overpopulation are symptoms. Two paths converge: keep growing enough to service financial claims (good for the economy, bad for nature), or depletion outruns innovation and financial claims 're-tether' to physical reality; his work is meeting that future halfway. Daniel asks viewers to watch Parts 1 and 2 first, since the story depends on Jevons paradox (tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox). HUMAN NATURE: evolutionary psychology is 'riddled' because it studies conditioned humans and calls it nature; cultures differ widely in violence and in valuing education (Jewish culture's education under persecution). Much of the problem is conditioning, not inexorable nature. Nate notes that certain, absolute messages ('climate will kill us all'; 'climate is a hoax') win attention; Daniel says platforms optimizing time-on-site use personalized AI to 'double down on the worst traits in humans' at trillion-dollar scale. REWARD CIRCUITS (tgs:Concept.OneMarshmallowRewardCircuits): the marshmallow experiment; supply-side economics strips the evolutionary reward from the work that earned it -- fast food (salt, fat and sugar without fiber or micronutrients; people dying of obesity are also micronutrient-starved), porn, social media -- giving 'pseudosatisfiers'; real friendships demand working through conflict and becoming a better person. The same holds for sensemaking: quick certainty is a one-marshmallow reward (tgs:Concept.PrematureCertainty), and in-group outrage hijacks belonging, security and esteem ('sanctimony'). The skill is earnestness, and willingness to sit in uncertainty outside a certain in-group. Nate asks whether that is rare; Daniel says statistically rare, not genetically limited -- otherwise democracy is irrelevant and we should make a few people the new nobility, 'the Chinese answer' (tgs:Concept.SensemakingPrerequisiteOfOpenSociety). Nate: the metanarrative must be assembled in an individual mind; you cannot put a climate, energy, AI and psychology expert in a room and assume everyone understands. REDUCTIONISM: coordination decomposes complex tasks and knowledge into disciplines, converting 'the complex to the complicated,' so we optimize a part at the expense of parts outside our domain -- iatrogenic medicine, chronic disease without cures, environmental fixes that ignore industry and economics. PUSHBACK: single-issue people (climate, factory farms, rainforests with orangutans burning 'to make paper bags,' police violence in the ghetto, Ukraine, Syria) live existential risk directly, so higher abstraction seems a privilege; Daniel says he and Nate came to systems work because none of the issues were improving, 'and maybe we're wrong.' Nate is disappointed others do not feel what he feels; Daniel says understanding needs grounding, takes time, and should serve people's existing work. WHOLENESS: Krishnamurti, 'the highest stage of intelligence is to observe without evaluation'; Bohm, the root of all problems is 'a fragmented consciousness' (tgs:Work.WholenessAndTheImplicateOrder); Einstein's 'optical delusion of consciousness.' Unknowing optimization of a part is mistake theory and externalities, intentional is conflict theory; across time, self, others, in-group and species, these are 'short-term pump and dumps' -- 'the generator function of the generator function' (tgs:Concept.FragmentedConsciousness). Nate: 'that was trippy,' and maybe growing the number of people who perceive from the whole matters more than refining the story. Daniel cites Bhagavad Gita 2.48 ('established in yoga, perform action') and 'seek ye first the kingdom.' EXPERIENCE: Nate's mystical experiences are in nature and he lives with grief daily; Daniel says the cognitive model came from experiences of sacredness and heartbreak, which must also be facilitated. Psychedelics can be 'one-marshmallows to spirituality'; he prefers deep time in nature. CATASTROPHIZING: many catastrophic-risk people have a catastrophizing disposition (CBT; Mark Twain), a gift for protecting but hard for collaboration; Daniel has an optimistic disposition, and fear and anger are 'epiphenomena' of loving something. Nate gets most pushback from people who say he is too optimistic; Daniel says premature optimism can be a spiritual bypass of grief -- people need to be met in it before asking about agency. DANIEL'S STORY: homeschooled, he did animal activism from age nine and worked with Greenpeace and PETA from 12 to 15, studying extinction and planetary-boundary metrics; he concluded self-induced extinction was inevitable and, as an 'emotionally immature' teenager, began thinking about technological depopulation -- one reason he now fears decentralized exponential technologies such as tabletop CRISPR. Buckminster Fuller's egg -- a developing bird uses up a finite egg white and fouls its shell, then breaks out -- showed him developmental periods end in discrete phase shifts his forecasting had ignored (tgs:Concept.DiscretePhaseShift). False certainty plus utilitarian, trolley-problem calculus justifies 'super fucked up stuff'; catastrophic and hopeful predictions (Y2K, the Cuban missile crisis, 2012) are both historically poor. The origin: as a child he looked into a cattle truck headed to slaughter, stopped eating animals that day and read Diet for a New America (tgs:Work.DietForANewAmerica); he describes despair so deep he wanted out, until he realized that would not help the animals, and his concern widened from factory farms to whaling, overfishing, clear-cutting and poverty. POST-TRAGIC: Marc Gafni's pre-tragic naivete, tragic cynicism, and post-tragic commitment to the sacredness of life 'whether you can succeed or not,' seeing the false certainty of both (tgs:Concept.PostTragic); even if every problem were solved -- an economy without an embedded growth obligation (tgs:Concept.EmbeddedGrowthObligation), closed-loop materials, AI bound -- a solar flare could end it, so one also holds fragility. Nate declines to return to egregores, attention and paperclip maximizers until next time. NEXT TIME: citizens are framed as consumers and voters, offered constrained choices that need no sensemaking; Daniel and Nate are not selling a party or product, and their approach is more 'two-marshmallow.' Part 4 will cover infrastructure, social structure and superstructure, exponential technology, and the design criteria of better systems (tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure), approached from 'for the thriving of life,' not against bad guys. Heart, will and mind must work together (tgs:Concept.HeartWillMind). Bertrand Russell (attribution uncertain): one studies philosophy for what it does to you; insight also serves 'the local PTA issue.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-18 from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the modeled-only entry. The prefLabel now carries the guest name, matching the site title 'Bend not Break #3'; the earlier rdfs:comment on Nate's Substack post and the YouTube replay link are kept (the show notes record no YouTube link). Spoken claims recorded but not checked: fossil energy drawn down '10 million times faster' than sequestered (Nate); the obesity and micronutrient link is supported by the show-note citation; Daniel himself flags the Bertrand Russell attribution as uncertain. Transcription artifacts: 'Mark Gaffney' is Marc Gafni (the show notes link his book Your Unique Self); 'abcess' is abscess; 'buzz' after 'what I meant' is unclear in the source. The show-note link labeled 'Doomerism' is broken (http://doomerism/). Sensitive personal content is summarized, not quoted: Daniel's teenage despair and his teenage thinking about depopulation, which he presents as a cautionary example of false certainty. Mentioned but not built: David Bohm (author of tgs:Work.WholenessAndTheImplicateOrder; not yet a Persona), J. Krishnamurti, Albert Einstein, Buckminster Fuller, Marc Gafni, John Robbins (author of tgs:Work.DietForANewAmerica), Mark Twain, Bertrand Russell, Greenpeace, PETA, the Bhagavad Gita. Candidate concepts named but NOT minted: reductionism converting the complex to the complicated (inside tgs:Concept.FragmentedConsciousness); mistake theory versus conflict theory (same); catastrophizing disposition and spiritual bypass of grief (inside tgs:Concept.PostTragic); consumers and voters as constrained choice (inside tgs:Concept.SensemakingPrerequisiteOfOpenSociety); psychedelics as one-marshmallow spirituality (inside tgs:Concept.OneMarshmallowRewardCircuits)."@en ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_71_DanielSchmachtenberger, tgs:Interview.TGS_50_CriteriaCategoriesResponse, tgs:Interview.TGS_42_ModelingDriversMetacrisis ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Greetings. Welcome to another episode of The Great Simplification. Today is part three with my friend and colleague Daniel Schmachtenberger, the founding director of The Consilience Project. This one went in a completely different direction than I expected. I was fully energized to have a back and forth discussion on my systems overview of the world... energy, ecology, money, systems... and see where Daniel agreed, differed, what we could converge on. Of course, it went in a completely different direction where we talked about meta-narratives themselves. Why they're threatening to various sections of society, how and why Daniel became a vegetarian, how to take in the entire systemic meta-crisis that humanity faces in a way that gives you agency and not despair. Whenever I finish one of these podcasts, I have a pretty good sense of whether I liked it, it was good. This one was fantastic. Though, you will see that my energy at the beginning kind of faded into speechlessness after some of the 15 paragraph thoughts that Daniel dropped on me. So, I hope you enjoy and learn and are inspired from this conversation with my friend, Daniel, which is turning into a longer series than I originally intended. And see you next week. Thanks."@en ;
    .


