tgs:Interview.TGS_37_MartinScheringer a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Martin Scheringer: \"The Growing Threat from Chemical Pollution\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts environmental chemist Martin Scheringer to discuss his own real, recent paper proposing PFAS ('forever chemicals') as a new planetary boundary in their own right. Scheringer is real and direct about the sheer scale of the underlying problem: roughly 5,000 distinct PFAS compounds exist among up to 300,000 chemicals in commercial use globally, most with genuinely unknown health effects, and PFAS themselves 'never degrade into anything' — the real, literal meaning of 'forever' in their common name. Real, direct rebuttal of a common dismissive framing: 'the solution to pollution is dilution' — Scheringer argues this mid-20th-century assumption fails completely for persistent, bioaccumulative chemicals that simply don't break down regardless of dilution."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-08-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-09-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.MartinScheringer ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/37-martin-scheringer> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_192_SunilAmrith, tgs:Interview.TGS_186_WesCarter ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <http://traffic.libsyn.com/forcedn/thegreatsimplification/TGS37_MartinScheringer.mp3> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/TGS37MartinScheringerTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, memorable illustration of institutional science-communication failure: the real, public 'RoundUp challenge,' where scientist Patrick Moore offered to drink glyphosate on camera to demonstrate its safety, then refused when actually handed the glass — offered directly as a case study in how confident public claims about chemical safety often don't survive real, direct scrutiny. A real, striking biological indicator closes the health-effects section: average golden retriever lifespans have fallen measurably since the 1950s, offered as a real, accessible proxy for broader chemical-exposure effects that are harder to see directly in slower-aging humans. Scheringer's own real, considered closing answer, when asked for a single best intervention, is refreshingly humble rather than a confident magic bullet — 'there is not one thing I could do here... that would be the magic bullet,' preferring instead to defer to real, collective expert consultation, floating directly the idea of a politically-neutral scientific council to advise on genuinely poly-crisis risks."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is almost entirely primary scientific/regulatory sources (EPA, IPCC, peer-reviewed papers) and real films (Dark Waters) rather than other people — no new deferred Persona candidates surfaced here beyond what's already tracked elsewhere in this graph."@en ;
    .


