tgs:Interview.TGS_42_ModelingDriversMetacrisis a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Daniel Schmachtenberger: \"Bend Not Break Part 4: Modeling the Drivers of the Metacrisis\""@en ;
    skos:definition "The fourth part of the Bend Not Break series, where Daniel and Nate merge their two lenses: Nate's energy-driven superorganism and Daniel's 'generator functions of catastrophic risk' are, Daniel says, 'the same thing using different lenses.' The episode lays the game-theoretic foundation of the series. Multipolar traps make individually rational behavior collectively fatal, like cancer cells (tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap); law can bind them within a nation but not between nations, so the world needs effective global governance that is not a world government (tgs:Concept.GlobalGovernanceWithoutGlobalGovernment). Humans escaped the co-selection that balances the rest of nature by building abstractions on abstractions -- tools for atoms, fire for energy, language for information (tgs:Concept.RecursiveAbstraction). The result is cooperation inside groups and competition between them, both paid for by nature (tgs:Concept.PositiveSumInGroupNegativeSumToNature), and profit as 'localized syntropy and systemic entropy' (tgs:Concept.LocalizedSyntropySystemicEntropy). What wins game-theoretically is not what is really valuable (tgs:Concept.GameTheoreticVersusRealValue). Here Daniel first names the third attractor on TGS: a path between catastrophe and dystopia that is 'threading the eye of a needle' (tgs:Concept.ThirdAttractor). The new superstructure he calls for is 'I am because we are,' with the 'we' extended to all people and the biosphere (tgs:Concept.IAmBecauseWeAre). The close previews Part 5's grid of triage, transition and long-term responses."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-10-03"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-10-26"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/42-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/TGS42DanielSchmactenberger04Transcript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/tQkQrc3Ant4> ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.BendNotBreak ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 4 ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.RecursiveAbstraction, tgs:Concept.PositiveSumInGroupNegativeSumToNature, tgs:Concept.LocalizedSyntropySystemicEntropy,
        tgs:Concept.GlobalGovernanceWithoutGlobalGovernment, tgs:Concept.GameTheoreticVersusRealValue, tgs:Concept.IAmBecauseWeAre,
        tgs:Concept.ThirdAttractor, tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap, tgs:Concept.OpportunityRiskAsymmetry, tgs:Concept.EmbeddedGrowthObligation,
        tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure, tgs:Concept.ThreeByThreeResponseGrid, tgs:Concept.Superorganism,
        tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple, tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox, tgs:Concept.DunbarsNumber, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse,
        tgs:Concept.EnergySlaves, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis ;
    dct:references tgs:Series.BendNotBreak, tgs:Interview.TGS_5_BendNotBreakPart1, tgs:Interview.TGS_20_MaximumPowerHyperAgents,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_31_SensemakingUncertaintyPurpose, tgs:Series.GreatSimplificationAnimatedSeries, tgs:AnimatedVideo.Part1EnergyBlind,
        tgs:Work.EconomicsForTheFutureBeyondTheSuperorganism, tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Work.TheRighteousMind, tgs:Work.Nonzero ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "RECAP: Nate summarizes Parts 1-3 and the superorganism: an energy-hungry global economy that self-organizes around growth (tgs:Work.EconomicsForTheFutureBeyondTheSuperorganism). Daniel says his 'generator functions of catastrophic risk' describe the same thing through a different lens, and that the two lenses have now informed each other. GAME THEORY: the 'meta-perverse incentive' of opportunity over risk -- talk up how AI might cure cancer and everyone wants to move fast (tgs:Concept.OpportunityRiskAsymmetry). The multipolar trap is a property of the superorganism (tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap). Human presence on the biosphere looks like a cancer: cancer cells use resources and reproduce faster than other cells, and they peak just before the host dies. Nate asks whether the point is to make cancer cells self-aware; Daniel replies that each cell is acting rationally ('if I stop, the body still dies'). Cigarettes and CFCs were regulated even though their makers knew the harm early, but energy is different: it is 'the engine of creation for the economy as a whole,' so the vested interest is everything. GOVERNANCE: rule of law solves multipolar traps inside a nation but not between nations, where the only enforcement is war. Global commons need something like global governance, but a one-world government would be corruptible, so it must be a decentralized process with checks and balances (tgs:Concept.GlobalGovernanceWithoutGlobalGovernment). On sovereignty, Daniel recalls their conversation with Samo asking how many 'real countries' can make strategic decisions; Nate answers about ten (US, China, Russia, France, Germany, UK and a couple of others). No country will lead on pricing oil properly. Nate suggests simplifying first; Daniel points to the gap in time to return on investment, and says an agreement between the US and China could be enough. INDIVIDUALS: be thoughtful about complicity but do not try to be perfect, or 'you will be in the forest'; Daniel cites Ubuntu, 'I am because we are' -- no one can be in perfect ethical alignment apart from helping the species move. The economy's accounting is wrong: no top industry would be profitable if it paid for the natural capital it uses. DEEP HISTORY: other species change through slow mutation and co-selection (a faster predator selects faster prey), but humans build adaptive abstractions on top of each other and became apex predators in every environment before agriculture; Nate credits John Gowdy and colleagues with dating the superorganism to agriculture about 10,000 years ago (tgs:Concept.RecursiveAbstraction). Ultrasociality applied to coordination (tgs:Work.RealityBlind; tgs:Work.TheRighteousMind; tgs:Concept.DunbarsNumber). Language 'accelerated localized syntropy and systemic entropy,' and so does profit -- privatized gains and socialized losses (tgs:Concept.LocalizedSyntropySystemicEntropy). The agrarian revolution was several jumps: goat herding (already desertifying), then the plow and animal husbandry with storage, then irrigation. The animist tribe that refused to yoke animals may have lived happier lives, but it was outcompeted. Robert Wright's Nonzero (tgs:Work.Nonzero) tells history as ever more positive-sum cooperation; Daniel adds that it was positive-sum within the group, zero-sum between groups and negative-sum to nature (tgs:Concept.PositiveSumInGroupNegativeSumToNature). INDUSTRY AND FINANCE: the carbon pulse (tgs:Series.GreatSimplificationAnimatedSeries; tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse); 'about 500 billion human labor equivalents' of fossil work (tgs:Concept.EnergySlaves), a number Daniel once called gibberish; population under half a billion from agriculture until industry, a first billion around 1815, eight billion now. The financial system has 'its own physics': compounding interest gives exponential growth, and real goods are indexed to it (tgs:Concept.EmbeddedGrowthObligation). Olivia Lazard's phrase: in decoupling from carbon 'we will recouple our economy to materials' (lithium up 900%). Rich countries appear to beat the Garrett relation and improve their Gini coefficient by importing goods made elsewhere, sometimes by slave labor; the average American uses 57 barrels of oil equivalent a year, plus 17 embodied in imports. THE THIRD ATTRACTOR (about 1:18): two attractor states are most likely, catastrophes and dystopias; the third must prevent the catastrophes with a coordination system that is 'not corruptible or capturable' -- 'threading the eye of a needle' (tgs:Concept.ThirdAttractor). WHAT IS UNIQUE ABOUT SAPIENS: what was selected was the group with its tech stack, social structure and superstructure (Marvin Harris, tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure). Humans are not apex predators -- compare a polar bear on a rampage with the Tsar Bomba -- and having broken the symmetry of power with everything else, we must consciously steward what no other animal has to; 'our adaptive capacity has surpassed the process by which evolution usually creates adaptive capacity, [so] our motive also has to transcend normal evolutionary motive' (tgs:Concept.IAmBecauseWeAre). Nate asks whether change comes from education, culture or elites; Daniel says all of them, since popular pressure and decision-makers inform each other, and calls for 'a cultural enlightenment' that ends 'if I don't, they're going to, so I have to.' VALUE: no one pays for the atmosphere, so cutting trees makes individual sense; what we put dollars into is not what we value, and 'game theoretical value and real value have a major delta' -- the needed change lowers relative game-theoretic power but could raise everyone's quality of life (tgs:Concept.GameTheoreticVersusRealValue). 'That or bust,' which Daniel maps to bend or break. SUMMARY: atoms, energy and bits are tightly coupled; software growth is another S-curve, limited by human attention and either feeding the materials economy (with Jevons paradox, tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox) or a 'race to the bottom of the brain stem' (Tristan Harris); sustained advantage is binding all three with coordination. Incentives alone extract everything; a national park exists only because a deterrent (law) stops the logging incentive, and there are global markets without global governance. Nate adds a financial 'musical chairs moment' when prior claims can no longer be serviced; Daniel says the domains interact, and climate migration is already a tipping point well before biosphere limits. Nate criticizes Fukuyama's tweets anticipating a Russian collapse, given its nuclear arsenal; Daniel warns that one-world-government advocates underweight its dangers and names a deep generator function: narrowly defined problems whose solutions externalize harm. Nate adds social sorting -- deferring to the highest-status person. Governance designed in 1776 for polymath representatives cannot handle today's complexity. BREADCRUMBS: near-term risks (war including nuclear, AI, bioweapons, planetary boundaries, supply chains); three horizons -- triage, transition (Larry Lessig's electoral reforms, B Corp-style fiduciary law) and long-term (perhaps networks of city-states, regulation that keeps pace with exponential tech, rethinking open publication of dangerous gene sequences) -- across infrastructure, social structure and superstructure. Nate asks whether that is a three-by-three grid; Daniel suggests a third axis of hyper-agents, egregores and institutions (tgs:Concept.ThreeByThreeResponseGrid). Daniel closes with a night-long recurring dream of a movement that held water, and then all the flows of life, as sacred. Nate frames the choice as life or growth; Daniel says growth need not stop but must move to another dimension -- quality of life personally, interpersonally and transpersonally -- in a way that is not outcompeted by the 'violence, domination, extraction, profit stack.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-18 from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the modeled-only entry (the prefLabel now carries the guest name, matching the site title 'Bend not Break #4'). This is the earliest TGS episode in the graph where Daniel names the third attractor; TGS_71 returns to it. Spoken claims recorded but not checked: the '10 real countries' list is Nate's, offered in reply to a question Daniel credits to 'Samo' (presumably Samo Burja; not built); population 'under half a billion' and a first billion 'at 1815' are spoken figures (the show notes link Our World in Data without confirming them); the 57 + 17 barrels per American figure is linked in the show notes to a general EJOLT article on endosomatic and exosomatic energy; Nate's '500 billion' fossil labor equivalents is linked to Reality Blind, and Daniel's 'gibberish' remark is his own aside. Transcription artifacts: 'centropy' is syntropy; 'eciency' is efficiency; 'positive-some' is positive-sum; 'Tsar bomb' is Tsar Bomba. The show-note link for the 'financial musical chairs moment' (youtu.be/hYQDEa99pYU) is not identified in the graph. Mentioned but not linked: John Gowdy (tgs:Persona.JohnGowdy, credited by Nate for dating the superorganism to agriculture). Mentioned but not built: Samo, Robert Wright (author of tgs:Work.Nonzero; not to be confused with tgs:Persona.RonaldWright), Olivia Lazard (later TGS guest, TGS_58), Tristan Harris (tgs:Persona.TristanHarris; the 'race to the bottom of the brain stem' is his phrase), Francis Fukuyama, Larry Lessig, Klaus Schwab (The Fourth Industrial Revolution, show-note link only), Steven Pinker and Hans Rosling (show-note link only), Metcalfe's law, limited liability companies, the Pareto distribution, Porcupine Tree. Candidate concepts named but NOT minted: the cancer-cell analogy (inside tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap usage here); energy as 'the engine of creation' versus single-product regulation (inside tgs:Concept.GlobalGovernanceWithoutGlobalGovernment); the coupling of atoms, energy and bits (inside tgs:Concept.RecursiveAbstraction); software growth as an attention-limited S-curve; narrow problem definition as the deepest generator function (close to tgs:Concept.HolisticExternalityAnalysis, TGS_50); growth moving to another dimension."@en ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_102_ThisWeeksLearnings, tgs:Monologue.TGS_187_EarthDay2025, tgs:Interview.TGS_71_DanielSchmachtenberger, tgs:Interview.TGS_50_CriteriaCategoriesResponse ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Here is the long awaited fourth installment in a series with Daniel Schmachtenberger. We dive deeper into the nuances of humans with our relationship with energy, materials and technology. How does technology intensify the multipolar trap on a world order based on growth? Is there a way out or could global solutions just make the problem worse? We closed this fourth conversation queuing up what our eventual goal was, which is a what to do framework on bend and not break for society in coming decades. Once again, here is Daniel Schmachtenberger."@en ;
    .


