tgs:Interview.TGS_48_TomasBjorkman a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tomas Björkman: \"The Great Transformation – Metamodernism and The Future\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with Swedish social entrepreneur Tomas Björkman, centered on his own real, championed philosophical framework of 'metamodernism' — a genuine synthesis moving past postmodernism's fragmentation toward renewed shared meaning without simply returning to pre-modern certainty. Björkman traces his own real intellectual and career arc directly — from physics and mathematics through investment banking to founding the Ekskäret Foundation (already built) — arguing more mindful, genuinely engaged global citizens are a real, necessary precondition for navigating civilization's coming challenges, not a soft addition to more material solutions."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-11-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-12-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.TomasBjorkman ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/48-tomas-bjorkman> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <http://traffic.libsyn.com/forcedn/thegreatsimplification/TGS48_TomasBjorkman.mp3> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/TGS48TomasBjorkmanTranscript.docx1_.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.TheMarketMyth, tgs:Work.TheNordicSecret, tgs:Work.TheWorldWeCreate, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_23_MordorEconomy, tgs:Interview.TGS_214_TristanHarris ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, striking statistic anchors Björkman's critique of modern markets directly: roughly 85% of exchange value in today's global economy consists of immaterial property rights rather than physical goods, offered as real, direct evidence for how detached financial abstraction has become from physical reality — with Disney's own real, repeated copyright-term extensions (informally dubbed the 'Mickey Mouse' extensions) cited as a concrete illustration. His own real, historical case study grounds the Inner Development Goals framework's plausibility: the real Nordic 'Bildung' tradition of folk high schools and adult retreat centers, credited by Björkman as producing the civic and personal maturity underlying Nordic countries' own real social outcomes — with a real, striking direct link drawn to Rosa Parks, who attended a real, similar Bildung-style institution, the Highlander Folk School in Tennessee, before her role in the Montgomery bus boycott. A real, warm, unscripted personal moment closes the episode: Björkman's own magic-wand answer — tweaking social media algorithms away from pure engagement-profit toward collective sensemaking and expanded empathy — prompts Nate to directly connect it to Tristan Harris's own real 'ring to Mordor' framing from his own TGS appearance (already built), with Björkman agreeing the fix itself could be a genuinely small technical tweak, not a fundamental reinvention. Closes on real, direct mutual energization rather than a standard sign-off: Nate discloses having slept poorly the night before, but describes the conversation itself as 'turbocharging' him, crediting the real, simple fact of finding someone on a different continent working toward the same goal."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Lene Rachel Andersen (real, direct co-author of The Nordic Secret, not built); this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic (Bildung tradition, Dunbar's number, metamodernism itself) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_50_CriteriaCategoriesResponse ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_49_SimonMichaux a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Simon Michaux: \"The Arcadians\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real second conversation with Simon Michaux, recorded on Halloween (both real, direct references to costumes open the episode — Michaux in a Superman shirt), building a preliminary real framework for societal response to the biophysical constraints laid out in their first conversation (TGS_19). Introduces Michaux's own real, distinctive four-part social taxonomy — 'the Arcadians' — describing how society is dividing into four parallel groups by paradigm as constraints intensify: the 'Old School' (denial, business-as-usual, roughly two-thirds to three-quarters of society currently, down from a real, estimated 95% two decades ago), the 'Vikings' (those who understand the old system is ending but choose short-term extraction over rebuilding), the 'Realists' (prepper-minded, focused on one-to-five-year survival needs), and the 'Arcadians' — the group Michaux places both himself and Nate within — thinking in 50-to-100-year timescales about genuinely rebuilding a wiser, more sustainable society, explicitly for the benefit of generations who will do the work without living to see its outcome."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-10-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-12-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.SimonMichaux ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/49-simon-michaux> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <http://traffic.libsyn.com/forcedn/thegreatsimplification/TGS49_SimonMichaux.mp3> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/TGS49_SimonMichaux.docx2_.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Michaux directly credits a real conversation with Steve Keen (already a known, deferred citation elsewhere in this graph via TGS #30) as the origin of the 'Vikings' category specifically — those who recognize the old system's collapse but choose short-term extraction over collective rebuilding, a real, distinct social response Michaux believes will prove self-limiting once there's nothing left to take. Michaux's own real, structural proposal reprojects Maslow's hierarchy of needs onto entire systems rather than individuals: real, distinct 'hierarchies of need' for energy, food, water, sewage/sanitation, heating, and industrial capacity, arguing modern society has forgotten these are industrially and technologically delivered rather than simply given, and that industry itself — mining, smelting, manufacturing — is wrongly treated as an afterthought despite being the real, physical foundation everything else depends on. A real, direct connection to Nate's own prior work closes the conceptual section: Nate connects the Arcadian framing directly to 'The Bottlenecks of the 21st Century' (his own real first book, co-written with DJ White, already built in this graph), framing the show's entire purpose as growing the real number of people thinking in Arcadian terms. Real, concrete personal confirmation closes the episode: Nate directly credits an actual, completed in-person visit to Michaux ('your actions when you came over on August 8th were amazing, and they have legs') — fulfilling the specific plan mentioned at the close of their first conversation five months earlier."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Steve Keen (real TGS episode #30, directly cross-referenced, credited directly with originating the 'Vikings' framing in conversation with Michaux) — already a known, deferred citation elsewhere in this graph."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_50_CriteriaCategoriesResponse a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Daniel Schmachtenberger: \"Bend Not Break Part 5: Criteria and Categories for Response\""@en ;
    skos:definition "The fifth and final part of the Bend Not Break series, turning from the problem set of Parts 1-4 to how to think about responding. Daniel deliberately offers ways of thinking rather than a list of solutions: stay high-touch while thinking at scale (tgs:Concept.HighTouchGrounding); observe and orient before jumping to solutions; analyze externalities upstream, downstream and psychosocially before acting (tgs:Concept.HolisticExternalityAnalysis); expect no single solution, since 'the answer to all of the problems is all of the solutions' (tgs:Concept.EcologyOfTheoriesOfChange); and, for the young, keep overhead low to buy back time (tgs:Concept.EmbeddedGrowthObligation). The episode closes on the 3x3 grid previewed at the end of Part 4 -- culture, political economy and technology against triage, transition and long-term horizons (tgs:Concept.ThreeByThreeResponseGrid) -- with worked examples from biosecurity, the Amazon's hydrological pump, Ecuador's debt, and US electoral reform. Nate's own focus, he says, is triage: preparing for the financial-energy 'snapback' and a smaller pie for eight billion people."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-11-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-12-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/50-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/TGS50DanielSchmactenberger05Transcript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/Kep8Fi_rUUI> ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.BendNotBreak ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 5 ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.ThreeByThreeResponseGrid, tgs:Concept.HolisticExternalityAnalysis, tgs:Concept.EcologyOfTheoriesOfChange,
        tgs:Concept.HighTouchGrounding, tgs:Concept.EmbeddedGrowthObligation, tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure,
        tgs:Concept.PredicamentNotProblem, tgs:Concept.PrecautionaryPrinciple, tgs:Concept.OpportunityRiskAsymmetry,
        tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.TheoryOfChange, tgs:Concept.TragedyOfTheCommons ;
    dct:references tgs:Series.BendNotBreak, tgs:Interview.TGS_5_BendNotBreakPart1, tgs:Interview.TGS_20_MaximumPowerHyperAgents,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_31_SensemakingUncertaintyPurpose, tgs:Interview.TGS_42_ModelingDriversMetacrisis,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_48_TomasBjorkman, tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Work.TheOverspentAmerican, tgs:Work.TragedyOfTheCommons1968 ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "RECAP: Nate frames the series as the logic of energy, materials, money and technology combining into an embedded exponential growth that heads toward an inflection point -- keep growing and environmental impact rises; stop and there is a 'Wile E. Coyote' moment -- and their shared aim as meeting the future halfway so civilization bends rather than breaks. Daniel gives shorthand for the three layers from the end of Part 4 -- culture, political economy and tech -- crediting Marvin Harris's cultural materialism, drawing on Marx (tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure). Parts 1 and 2-4 framed the problem; Part 3 was 'a psychological interlude' on holding catastrophic awareness with agency. PRECONDITIONS: everyone's work matters, including the nurse, the firefighter and the animal-shelter worker, since the metacrisis is caused by everyone's cumulative activity and must be addressed the same way. Different typologies need different advice (Warren Buffett versus Steve Jobs). Daniel's own path to systems work began in animal shelters, and he distrusts existential-risk thinkers who are not 'high-touch' (tgs:Concept.HighTouchGrounding); Nate observes that Daniel's own trouble saying no limits his reach. HOW NOT TO THINK ABOUT SOLUTIONS: an elite counter-terrorism unit he advised had to train operators out of jumping to known drills and into assessing the novelty of each situation -- the 'observe, orient' of the OODA loop -- and comfort with uncertainty lets you observe well. Institutional mandates can be 'a corrupt choice-making architecture' even for nonprofits, if narrow metrics let you harm what you do not measure. Nate's analysis-heavy career is affirmed as the right decide-and-act for him; OODA loops should live in networks of people with different roles. EXTERNALITIES: a list of questions for a problem space (Daniel offers to send it for the show notes): where are the perverse incentives, financial, status or cover-your-ass; what is upstream (the elephant-poaching example: poachers still poor simply moved to rhinos); what is downstream across second-to-nth-order effects. Nate notes an iPhone would cost $5,000 with externalities included; Daniel says the market as it exists depends on externalizing cost. Richard Haass's distinction between problems and predicaments; Nate now says 'responses' rather than 'solutions' (tgs:Concept.PredicamentNotProblem). Bohm and Krishnamurti: the root cause is fragmented consciousness, which could not steward exponential power. A culture that recognizes interconnection is needed for a political economy that is 'the will of the people' to bind the economics that direct the tech. Intrinsic as well as extrinsic motivation. The method (tgs:Concept.HolisticExternalityAnalysis): talk to many perspectives including opponents, red-team and refine, run safe-to-fail probes, listen broadly after scaling, keep governance able to update; physical versus psychosocial externalities, the latter including political messaging that radicalizes the audience it does not target. Why harms get externalized: benefits now and harms later, game-theoretic advantage to whoever takes the risk, the epistemic ease of first-order effects, tragedy of the commons, and the opportunity-versus-risk asymmetry (tgs:Concept.OpportunityRiskAsymmetry), worsened by limited liability. Peaceful, prosperous places can fall into extremism (Iran, Liberia, Syria, Ukraine). THE RATE LIMITER: people who are high-touch and also able to think strategically at scale; Daniel mentions training modules and an academy in development. NO SINGLE SOLUTION: everyone he asked named a different root (economics, education, media, governance, political economy, parenting, the environment) and 'they were all right' -- 'there is not a theory of change, there's an ecology of theories of change' (tgs:Concept.EcologyOfTheoriesOfChange). ADVICE: keep overhead low and buy back time; do not put an embedded growth obligation on your own earnings (tgs:Concept.EmbeddedGrowthObligation; Nate cites Juliet Schor's finding that people always want to earn more next year); be willing to change jobs; invest in therapy and personal development; find mentors and make yourself useful to them. Nate: degrowth is what we should do, post-growth is what we will have to do. THE GRID (tgs:Concept.ThreeByThreeResponseGrid): triage examples are biosecurity regulation and protecting the Amazon's hydrological pump by recruiting the US State Department on food-security grounds; transition examples are a bundled, exclusive deal for Ecuador (debt relief, development and protection together) and Larry Lessig's electoral reforms; long-term means real-cost pricing, new education, closed-loop production and governance built for exponential technology. Nate says his own focus is triage for a 'financial energy rubber band snapback.' They propose a follow-up episode on audience questions, and Daniel suggests community watch groups for the five episodes."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-18 from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the modeled-only entry (prefLabel now carries the guest name, matching the site title). Nate's intro calls it the final installment, with a possible number six for audience questions; that follow-up is not identified here (TGS_71 was the next Schmachtenberger episode, but on AI rather than questions). Spoken claims recorded but not checked: Daniel says the Amazon's moisture supplies 'between a third and a half' of North American rainfall -- the show notes link a general article on how Amazon deforestation affects weather, which does not confirm the figure; Daniel flags his Ecuador debt figures ('$32 billion') as possibly wrong. The list of problem-space questions Daniel offered for the show notes appears there only as a heading with no link. Transcription artifacts: 'Julia Schor' is Juliet Schor; 'Rachel Carlson' is Rachel Carson; 'Noah' is NOAA; 'extragenic' is iatrogenic; 'the con' is the Congo; 'ligi laboratories' is unclear in the source. Mentioned and already in the graph: Marvin Harris (tgs:Persona.MarvinHarris, credited on tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure). Mentioned but not built: Dave Snowden, Richard Haass, Larry Lessig, Nelson Del Rio (Prosperity of the Commons), David Bohm and J. Krishnamurti. Candidate concepts named but NOT minted: observe-orient before deciding (inside tgs:Concept.HolisticExternalityAnalysis); psychosocial externalities (same); bundling with exclusivity as a market tool (inside tgs:Concept.ThreeByThreeResponseGrid); intrinsic versus extrinsic motivation; fragmented consciousness (since minted as tgs:Concept.FragmentedConsciousness, from TGS_31)."@en ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_71_DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "We have arrived at episode five in the series with my friend and colleague, Daniel Schmachtenberger, called Bend Not Break. In this final episode of this series, we do an overview of short-term, intermediate term, long-term, and culture political economy infrastructure, A three by three grid on how do we think about responding to the upcoming meta- crisis, to the upcoming Great Simplification. At the end, Daniel and I invite viewers to put questions in the comment section on YouTube for what we might do a follow-up episode on. This was quite a journey. We wended did our way through the fifth episode, and I hope you enjoy it. Without any further ado, here is Daniel."@en ;
    .

tgs:Interview.TGS_51_DJWhite a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "DJ White: \"Ocean Effectivism\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first podcast conversation with DJ White — co-founder, Greenpeace International; founder, EarthTrust — despite a genuine, extensive 15-year pre-existing friendship and collaboration predating this recording by over a decade. Nate's own opening traces DJ's real, extraordinary career: co-founding Greenpeace, confronting Soviet whalers in 1976, creating the first international dolphin-friendly tuna labeling regime, ending the global driftnet fishing fleet in the 1980s, stopping a national dolphin drive kill later dramatized (without credit) in the film The Cove, and — with direct real relevance to this graph's own origins — leading the real EarthTrust team that broke through blockades during the 1991 Kuwaiti oil fires. The two originally met as fellow commenters on The Oil Drum roughly 15 years before this recording."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-10-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-12-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DJWhite ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/51-dj-white> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.TGS_187_EarthDay2025, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_82_PowerVsLife, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_67_TheSolutionsThatCanBeNamedAreNotTheSolutions, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_81_TimeTravelAndTheSuperorganism, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_90_NetZeroAndOtherDelusions, tgs:Interview.TGS_170_KristineTompkins ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <http://traffic.libsyn.com/forcedn/thegreatsimplification/TGS51_DJWhite_1.mp3> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/TGS51DJWhiteTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "White traces his own real origin story to a formative epiphany in a Louisiana salt marsh: after years of consciously living cheaply to fund donations to wildlife causes rather than personal spending, he quit a career as an oil geophysicist the next day to pursue ocean activism full-time, having never yet met a whale or dolphin in person. A real, formative personal bond anchors his own account of what sustained decades of largely uncredited work: a real, direct relationship with a specific dolphin he came to know well, which he describes as having permanently shaped both his life's direction and a private promise he made to her personally. White is real and direct about the human cost of his own approach to activism — describing himself, at nearly 72, as genuinely worried he may live to see everything he's ever fought for come to nothing regardless of any individual campaign's success, given the larger trajectory he sees unfolding. He closes not with false optimism but with a real, considered case for meaning independent of outcome: campaign veterans he stays in contact with decades later consistently describe the work itself, win or lose, as the most significant thing they've ever done — offered directly as evidence that committing fully to a difficult, uncertain effort has real value distinct from whether it ultimately succeeds. Real, direct credit: White originated the show's own 'TaaL' (Through an Alien Lens) framing device, used throughout Nate's books, for genuinely reframing familiar problems by deliberately connecting ideas that wouldn't normally be associated."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, notable new detail surfacing here, not previously captured in this graph: the three books DJ White has co-authored with Nate are used together in a real, specific class Nate teaches called 'Reality 101' — the specific title of the third book (beyond Reality Blind and The Bottlenecks of the 21st Century) remains genuinely unconfirmed, still an open question for a future pass. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Rex Weyler (already a real, built Persona in this graph via RR-2) is directly, independently reconfirmed here as a real Vancouver Greenpeace colleague who preceded White; John C. Lilly (real, directly cited dolphin-cognition researcher); Aza Raskin (real TGS episode #22, Earth Species Project), now built (2026-09-02)."@en ;
    .


