tgs:Interview.TGS_70_JohnKitzhaber a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "John Kitzhaber: \"What Makes a Healthy Society?\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts John Kitzhaber — former ER physician, three-term Governor of Oregon, and architect of the real Oregon Health Plan and Coordinated Care Organizations — for a direct critique of US healthcare as a real, structural case study in misallocated resources. US healthcare spending approaches 20% of GDP, roughly $12,900 per capita annually, while producing real, measurably worse outcomes than other developed nations — the highest infant mortality rate and lowest life expectancy among OECD peers."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-04-13"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-05-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JohnKitzhaber ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/70-john-kitzhaber> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z4cjl77rj78> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/TGS70JohnKitzhaberTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_154_JaneMuncke ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Kitzhaber directly traces the Oregon Health Plan's own real origin to a real, painful case: Coby Howard, a young Oregon boy whose Medicaid-funded bone marrow transplant was denied under the state's existing rationing rules, a real, public controversy that directly forced the state to confront resource allocation explicitly rather than implicitly. He's real and direct about US healthcare's structural misdirection: roughly 25 cents of every healthcare dollar produces no measurable positive outcome, while real social determinants — poverty, early-life investment, nutrition — account for the large majority of lifetime health status, with formal healthcare system contact itself responsible for only about 10%. A real, striking regulatory-capture statistic anchors his institutional critique: roughly a quarter of FDA employees go on to work for or consult with pharmaceutical companies, and about two-thirds of retired 2019 members of Congress who took jobs outside politics moved into lobbying — real, direct evidence of the same revolving-door dynamic driving healthcare policy broadly. Closes on real, direct personal reflection distinct from policy analysis: asked what he cares about most, Kitzhaber points directly to wanting to leave a healthy planet and society for his own 25-year-old son's generation, citing Thích Nhất Hạnh's real, direct metaphor of navigating by the North Star — not expecting to reach it, but using it to hold a consistent direction across a lifetime of uncertain progress. His own real, unhedged magic-wand answer is not policy but disposition: helping people momentarily set aside polarization to remember two things everyone genuinely shares — mortality, and the same underlying desire for meaning, value, and a fair opportunity to pursue their own path."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is almost entirely statistical/institutional (CMS, OECD, FDA revolving-door reporting) rather than pointing to further individuals — no new deferred Persona candidates surfaced here."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_71_DanielSchmachtenberger a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Daniel Schmachtenberger: \"Artificial Intelligence and The Superorganism\""@en ;
    skos:definition "A three-hour conversation in which Nate and Daniel Schmachtenberger merge the two lines of thought their friendship began with -- Nate's energy-money-technology superorganism and Daniel's focus on existential risk from emerging AI -- set aside from their Bend Not Break series. Underneath the AI discussion runs a deeper question, which Nate names in his intro: what intelligence is, and how intelligence in groups has out-competed the wisdom and restraint of other cultures. Daniel's central claims: intelligence is the capacity to achieve goals, and narrow goal-achieving, not growth, is the underlying generative dynamic, with growth as its epiphenomenon (tgs:Concept.NarrowGoalAchieving); technology is not values-neutral, because whatever confers competitive advantage becomes obligate (tgs:Concept.TechnologyIsNotValuesNeutral); AI is not one more risk within the metacrisis but an accelerant of all of them (tgs:Concept.AIAsMetacrisisAccelerant); and what must bind intelligence is wisdom (tgs:Concept.IntelligenceBoundByWisdom). Here Daniel returns to the third attractor, first named on TGS in Part 4 of Bend Not Break (TGS_42): neither catastrophe from uncoordinated races nor dystopia from centralized control (tgs:Concept.ThirdAttractor). Nate ends by apologizing that for four years he thought limits to growth dominated AI as a risk."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-05-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-05-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/71-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/TGS71DanielSchmachtenbergerTranscript.docx1_.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_P8PLHvZygo> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.ThirdAttractor, tgs:Concept.IntelligenceBoundByWisdom, tgs:Concept.NarrowGoalAchieving,
        tgs:Concept.NaiveProgress, tgs:Concept.TechnologyIsNotValuesNeutral, tgs:Concept.AIAsMetacrisisAccelerant,
        tgs:Concept.OpportunityRiskAsymmetry, tgs:Concept.RiskSingularity, tgs:Concept.OmniLose,
        tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap, tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox,
        tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple, tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative, tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries,
        tgs:Concept.CorporationsAsMisalignedAI ;
    dct:references tgs:Series.BendNotBreak, tgs:Interview.TGS_5_BendNotBreakPart1, tgs:Interview.TGS_20_MaximumPowerHyperAgents,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_31_SensemakingUncertaintyPurpose, tgs:Interview.TGS_42_ModelingDriversMetacrisis,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_50_CriteriaCategoriesResponse, tgs:Interview.TGS_65_AntonioTuriel,
        tgs:Work.EconomicsForTheFutureBeyondTheSuperorganism, tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Work.TheMasterAndHisEmissary,
        tgs:Work.TechnologyIsNotValuesNeutral ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "FRAMING: Nate recaps the superorganism -- a global economy organized around profit, needing energy, materials and technology, with decision-making outsourced to a market blind to its impacts, money as a claim on energy, and a growth imperative built into debt-based money creation -- and lists his AI worries: the end of shared truth, a 'turbo button' on the superorganism feeding Jevons paradox, and lost jobs. Daniel notes ChatGPT's unprecedented adoption speed, and that corporate races, adoption curves and dependencies make it irretractable. PROGRESS NARRATIVES: Daniel describes cognitive-bias clusters around techno-optimism and techno-pessimism (tgs:Concept.NaiveProgress); Nate adds the two blind spots of naive progress, its costs and the one-time subsidy of non-renewable inputs, and calls Daniel's alternative a wide-boundary definition of progress. Institutions measured on narrow metrics (a UN hunger program, say) can't avoid externalizing harm to soil and pollinators. Naive pessimism loses too: a culture that refuses an advantage-conferring technology loses wars and growth (the plow and animism, Tibet and China, Genghis Khan and Alexander), so technology becomes obligate (tgs:Concept.TechnologyIsNotValuesNeutral). Nate wonders whether communities that simplify first, such as an energy-poor Europe (citing TGS_65 guest Antonio Turiel), lose now but learn first; Daniel answers with the multipolar trap: 'either everybody has to or nobody can,' since pricing resources honestly prices you out of the market. Industrial technology made environmental destruction global for the first time, so 'it can no longer be that the winners can win at the expense of everybody else.' Daniel is a techno-optimist only post-naive and post-cynical: the same many-to-many technology without an ad model could have optimized for development and connection instead of engagement. Nate offers the golden retriever as humanity's best technology. ORDERS OF EFFECT AND MODELS: Nate explains Jevons paradox (efficiency up 36%, energy use up 63%); Daniel generalizes it to first versus second-to-nth-order effects -- 'it is logically, cognitively easier... to figure out how to achieve a goal than it is to make sure that goal doesn't fuck other stuff up' -- and calls optimization itself the wrong framework. Intelligence is defined pragmatically as goal-achieving; what is distinct in humans is recursive abstraction leading to recursive innovation, beginning with stone tools (matter), fire (energy) and language (information). Models help and also blind: the Tao Te Ching's 'the Tao that can be spoken is not the eternal Tao,' and nutrients being non-fungible (people dying of obesity are dying of malnutrition) warn against reducing everything to energy. Models that win short-term wars spread regardless of truth (the Inquisition), leading to evolutionary cul-de-sacs. WISDOM AND RESTRAINT: indigenous stories (via Samantha Sweetwater and Tyson Yunkaporta) of the apex predator handing humans the mantle of stewardship; the Sabbath as law binding a multipolar trap; 'there's no definition of wisdom worth anything that is not bound to the concept of restraint' (Sweetwater); Chesterton's fence and the traditional-progressive dialectic; values held in dialectical tension. Humans evolved with the tribe as the unit of selection, malleable by nurture, neotenous; beyond Dunbar-scale groups, lying and sociopathy started to pay and the multipolar trap took off. 'Are we genetically misfit as beings to wisdom? No. Did we produce a civilization that is misfit to wisdom...? Yes' -- the superorganism, Moloch, the megamachine. Neither the romantic nor the Hobbesian picture of tribal life is true. MARKETS AS INTELLIGENCE: capitalism has no single origin (private property, surplus, currency, rent-seeking, banking); Adam Smith's invisible hand anthropomorphized emergent collective behavior as a good god. Narrow AI already surpasses humans beyond calibration (AlphaZero-style self-play beating Stockfish; swarm drones; high-frequency trading). Who owns AI decides its goals. Agreements are the way out of multipolar traps, as mutually assured destruction made winning equal losing, but they are hard to verify internationally; OpenAI's and Anthropic's trajectories show the race dynamic. All technology is dual-use, and AI is omni-use. Opportunity-focused builders outrun risk-focused ones (tgs:Concept.OpportunityRiskAsymmetry), using unknown unknowns as plausible deniability while privatizing gains. Nate senses a 'risk singularity,' and Daniel agrees (tgs:Concept.RiskSingularity). Daniel reframes Nate's growth driver: narrow goal-achieving is the generative dynamic, growth its epiphenomenon (tgs:Concept.NarrowGoalAchieving). AI applied to mining makes marginal deposits profitable. A centrally planned AI economy would trade catastrophe for dystopia; what is needed is a third attractor (tgs:Concept.ThirdAttractor). AGI risk: Yudkowsky on Bankless ('at the end we should at least be honest with each other'), instrumental convergence, why companies race anyway. CLOSING: 'Human intelligence, unbound by wisdom... is the cause of the meta crisis and the growth imperative of the superorganism'; AI is its recursive externalization, an accelerant of every risk (tgs:Concept.AIAsMetacrisisAccelerant). Any definable metric set leaves something out, so wisdom is 'the difference between... what the optimization function says you should do... and what you should actually do.' The market is already a general autonomous superintelligence running on humans; a superintelligence aligned with 'the thriving of all life in perpetuity' would need builders with that goal. Bohm (fragmentation and wholeness) and McGilchrist (the master and his emissary) frame the cure (tgs:Concept.IntelligenceBoundByWisdom). Nate apologizes for underrating AI and adds a role for education and a shift from me to we; Daniel warns that people who commune with wholeness but drop out don't change the curve either, while those who act on narrow goals without that connection repeat the problem -- hold both, with humility."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-18 from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the 2026-09-12 stub; title confirmed from the show notes. Nate describes it as 'probably one of the better conversations I've ever had' and 'three and a half hours' (runtime 3h12m). Daniel names 'the third attractor' here, but he first named it on TGS in TGS_42 (corrected 2026-10-18). Spoken claims vs show notes: Nate's efficiency figures are spoken 'since 1990' and the show notes say 'since 1990s'. Daniel recalls 'GPT-3' reaching 100 million users in about six weeks; that record was ChatGPT's. He recalls AlphaGo beating Stockfish 'roughly 38-0'; the self-play chess system was DeepMind's AlphaZero, and the result is not checked here. He first names 'Robert Wright' for the AI explainer videos and corrects himself to Robert Miles. Transcription artifacts: 'Goblectepi' is Gobekli Tepe; 'Sumaria' is Sumer; 'homage' is hominids; 'Mylan sheaths' is myelin sheaths; 'rival risk' is rivalrous; 'anthrop scene' is Anthropocene; 'Walter Stanley Jevons' is William Stanley Jevons (Nate's slip or a transcription error). Referenced but not in the graph: Nate's 2023 Earth Day talk; the Bohm-Krishnamurti conversations; Eliezer Yudkowsky on Bankless; Robert Miles's videos. Later guests mentioned in advance (not referenced, since they postdate this episode): Tyson Yunkaporta ('two weeks from now'), Iain McGilchrist (TGS_85) and Samantha Sweetwater (TGS_112). Candidate concepts named but NOT minted: evolutionary cul-de-sacs; omni-use technology (inside tgs:Concept.AIAsMetacrisisAccelerant); the market as an existing superintelligence (related to tgs:Concept.CorporationsAsMisalignedAI); instrumental convergence; the Sabbath as law binding a multipolar trap and Chesterton's fence (inside tgs:Concept.IntelligenceBoundByWisdom); externality forecasting."@en ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_204_KellyErhart, tgs:Interview.TGS_147_IndyJohar, tgs:Interview.TGS_132_WideBoundaryImpactsOfAI, tgs:Interview.TGS_126_AVisionForBetterment ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Artificial intelligence is in the news. We hear about ChatGPT, making people more efficient, learning quicker. We hear about AI replacing artists and mid-level programmers. We see deep fakes and fake beautiful baby peacocks that are much cuter than real baby peacocks. And lots of people are debating about the benefits and risks of artificial intelligence. But today's guest is my colleague and friend, Daniel Schmachtenberger, who is back for a deep dive on how artificial intelligence accelerates the superorganism dynamic with respect to extraction, climate and many of the planetary boundary limits that we face. I have not heard this angle on artificial intelligence before. I think it's really important to have this conversation and throughout this talk with Daniel, we talked about AI, but underpinning it all was what is intelligence and how has intelligence in groups in human history out-competed wisdom restraint of different cultures and different groups of humans? This is an intense, dense three and a half hour conversation and we weren't even done. We'll be back in the next month or so to have the follow on questions. It's probably one of the better conversations I've ever had on The Great Simplification, and I think it's really important to merge the environmental consequences of AI into our cultural discourse. Here's my friend Daniel Schmachtenberger."@en ;
    .

