tgs:Interview.TGS_132_WideBoundaryImpactsOfAI a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Daniel Schmachtenberger: \"Silicon Dreams and Carbon Nightmares: The Wide Boundary Impacts of AI\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Daniel Schmachtenberger's wide-boundary assessment of artificial intelligence's effect on climate and the environment, recorded as a follow-up to the earlier AI-and-the-superorganism conversation (TGS_71) and originally planned as a 30-minute stand-in for a Frankly. Against the conference narrative that AI will be a boon for climate, he argues that the claimed benefits are mostly marginal efficiency gains that trigger the Jevons paradox, while the transformative ones (fusion, room-temperature superconductors) have no precedent on any useful timeline. He organizes AI's environmental impact into three widening circles (tgs:Concept.ThreeCirclesOfAIEnvironmentalImpact), argues that new technology helps only when it is legally bound to replace the old (tgs:Concept.BindingTheJevonsParadox), and locates the engine beneath both the market and AI in game theory: maximizing returns on power (tgs:Concept.MaximizingReturnsOnPower), which he names as the heart of Moloch when Nate asks what the beating heart of the superorganism is. The opening twenty minutes, which Nate warns are intense, cover the AGI race, transhumanist metaphysics in Silicon Valley, ubiquitous technological surveillance and automated kill webs."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-06-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-07-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/132-daniel-schmachtenberger> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/TGS132_DanielSchmachtenberger-AI.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1NFuddEAi5s> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.ThreeCirclesOfAIEnvironmentalImpact, tgs:Concept.BindingTheJevonsParadox,
        tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure, tgs:Concept.MaximizingReturnsOnPower,
        tgs:Concept.OmniLose, tgs:Concept.PrecautionaryPrinciple, tgs:Concept.CorrelationOfForceAndMeans,
        tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox, tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple,
        tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries, tgs:Concept.StrawSiphonSieve, tgs:Concept.CorporationsAsMisalignedAI,
        tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative ;
    dct:references tgs:Series.BendNotBreak, tgs:Interview.TGS_16_TristanHarris, tgs:Interview.TGS_71_DanielSchmachtenberger,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_122_ZakStein, tgs:Interview.TGS_126_AVisionForBetterment, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_56_PeakOilAIAndTheStraw,
        tgs:Work.TacklingClimateChangeWithMachineLearning, tgs:Work.DevelopmentInProgress, tgs:Work.WhereArgumentsComeFrom, tgs:Work.HumanPurposeAndTranshumanPotential ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "THE CLAIMED UPSIDE: Daniel points to the paper 'Tackling Climate Change with Machine Learning' as the comprehensive list of ways AI could help -- better modeling of ice melt and atmosphere (including for aerosol geoengineering), efficiency gains such as sensor networks finding pipeline leaks, and breakthroughs in fusion, superconductors and batteries. What is solid is AI making sense of large sensor datasets; ironically, a major application is making oil extraction cheaper (92% of oil companies have contracts with AI firms; NVIDIA and Microsoft serve the industry). Breakthroughs are another matter: AI has long been used in fission with marginal gains, and there is no precedent for high confidence on any timeline. WHY THE STORY SELLS: AI is marketed to every sector (defense, law enforcement, education, elder care), and climate is an influential audience; the top companies by market cap are now AI companies with matching marketing and lobbying budgets, while environmentalists have the 'correlation of force and means' of peace activists against the military-industrial complex. Consilience's paper 'Where Arguments Come From' describes how ideas are upregulated by whoever can fund them. People also want to believe it: it is where the money is, and for the depressed climate-concerned it offers a rescuer, 'a regress to a childhood psyche, still wanting a parent.' THE RACE: the AI safety joke that the fastest way to accelerate AI risk is to start an AI safety org (OpenAI, then Anthropic, then Ilya Sutskever's new company); lab leaders publicly saying AI may kill everyone; and the 'inevitability' argument -- only one game, AGI supremacy, so whatever must be harmed to get there first is worth it -- which Daniel calls the superlative case of motivated reasoning he has seen, since every racer argues that they themselves should win. Behind it sits a transhumanist metaphysics (Bostrom, David Pearce's abolitionist project, Ted Chu, Nick Land, Kurzweil; Elon Musk's 'hydrocarbon life is the bootloader for silica life') in which replacing biology is a moral good. Asked about redwoods and bees, the honest accelerationist answer is that they are 'good sources of atoms' (Yudkowsky's framing), and the military answer is that only one game matters (Operation Hellscape in the Taiwan Strait, Anduril's automated kill webs). Ubiquitous technological surveillance -- Wi-Fi interference read by AI, the internet of things, daily high-resolution satellite imagery -- removes the old limit on autocracy that rebellion could foment unseen. Would 95% of humans be gnats? 'Not yet. But very soon'; if 95% woke up and acted it could not continue -- 'not hopeless, but urgent.' THE ENVIRONMENTAL CIRCLES: data centers use more energy than all but the top 16 countries and are the fastest-growing demand, straining grids (Virginia) and pushing companies to build their own nuclear plants; Microsoft's 2030 renewable pledge is slipping. Nate explains rebound versus backfire (efficiency up 36%, energy use up 63%). Daniel lays out the three circles (tgs:Concept.ThreeCirclesOfAIEnvironmentalImpact) and the PFAS example: about $16 trillion a year to internalize the cost of each year's use, versus an industry of $30-40 billion -- 'the only cost we pay is the cost we absolutely have to pay.' Fossil fuel use has risen nearly every year since the first climate paper in 1938, dipping only in recessions and COVID. THE FIX IS NOT TECH ALONE: renewables must displace, not add (tgs:Concept.BindingTheJevonsParadox); Nate adds that a 19-terawatt metabolism cannot simply be swapped out without changed aspirations, and Daniel names the move they just made -- from infrastructure up to social structure and superstructure (tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure). Fusion timelines keep slipping and would take decades to scale, longer than the window before tipping points; space colonization is no exit ('we have to actually get it right here first'). Nate likens AI to his shale 'bigger straw' (Frankly_56) and suggests its recursive energy demand may finally make society notice peak oil; Daniel replies that faster depletion and further 'Venusification' both lose, with Arctic oil and an estimated $50 trillion of Antarctic mineral assets coming into reach as ice melts. Organic food and veganism grew while total pesticide use and factory farming grew too: new markets add rather than replace. THE ENGINE: AI is built by for-profit companies with fiduciary duties, so it serves the market dynamics that need binding. Asked whether AI or markets are the beating heart of the superorganism, or 'Moloch,' Daniel answers maximizing returns on power (tgs:Concept.MaximizingReturnsOnPower), older than markets and potentially outliving them, since AI is the first technology that could replace finance itself. The front-runner in an arms race is the one who could bind it, and win-lose now becomes omni-lose (tgs:Concept.OmniLose). WHAT TO DO: think in correlation of force and means rather than single wins (tgs:Concept.CorrelationOfForceAndMeans); apply the precautionary principle (tgs:Concept.PrecautionaryPrinciple) -- 'if we do not get that, I don't think we make it'; press representatives to decelerate every war front that could lead to World War III and everything moving past points of irreversibility; and for environmentalists, study AI's real upsides and downsides so as not to place hope wrongly. Daniel closes that AI risk cannot be understood without energy and materiality, and that almost nobody but Nate is having that conversation; he proposes a future episode on regulation adequate to exponential technology, yellow teaming, and the embedded growth obligation."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-18 from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the modeled-only entry. Corrects that entry's scopeNote: this episode's own show notes (00:00, 'Prior episode on Artificial Intelligence', youtu.be/_P8PLHvZygo) and Nate's intro ('a followup to our original one on AI and the superorganism') confirm the reference to TGS_71, so it is now in dct:references. The show description points listeners to a document of AI resources compiled by the Civilization Research Institute (tgs:Organization.CivilizationResearchInstitute). Spoken figures vs show notes (show notes trusted): Nate says efficiency rose 36% while energy use rose 63% 'since 1990'; the show notes say since 1996. Daniel recalls the $10.3 billion PFAS settlement as 'I think it was Dow'; the widely reported $10.3 billion settlement (2023) was 3M's -- not verified against the show notes, which link only the PFAS cost study. Ted Chu's book is spoken as 'Transhumanism and Human Potential'; the show notes give 'Human Purpose and Transhuman Potential: A Cosmic Vision of Our Future Evolution.' Transcription artifacts: 'PFOS' is PFAS in most uses; 'Soromon' is Sauron; 'Andoril' is Anduril; 'Miriam' is MIRI (Machine Intelligence Research Institute); 'David Pierce' is David Pearce; 'Elias' is Ilya Sutskever; 'MOLOC' is Moloch; 'jewels' is joules; 'Microsoft Mechanics www. microsoft. com' is a stray insertion. Mentioned but not referenced: Janine Benyus, whose TGS episode (TGS_135) Nate says he recorded the day before but which was published after this one. Not built: Nick Bostrom, David Pearce, Ted Chu, Nick Land, Ray Kurzweil, Alex Karp, Palantir, Anduril, Planet Labs, Operation Hellscape. Candidate concepts named but NOT minted: ubiquitous technological surveillance; automated kill webs; the 'minimum unit of civilization' (a six-continent supply chain, so national metrics such as the Gini coefficient mislead); the AGI inevitability argument as motivated reasoning (recorded inside tgs:Concept.OmniLose); yellow teaming."@en ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_147_IndyJohar, tgs:Interview.TGS_184_ConnorLeahy ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "I would like to welcome my colleague Daniel Schmachtenberger back to the program to take a deep dive on artificial intelligence potential impact on the environment and particularly climate change. I have recently gone to some energy tech climate conferences where AI is in. Every other presentation, at least, talking about how it's going to reduce emissions and be a general boon for the environment. My gut and my conversations with Daniel, Tristan, Harris, and others tells me this is not likely the case. so, thank you. This conversation is a followup to our original one on AI and the superorganism. This was a deeply informative and oftentimes disturbing conversation, especially the 20 minutes, in the beginning, before we got to AI and climate, was talking about the broader macro story of artificial intelligence, human power systems, and what's happening, and some worst case scenarios. So you. Be warned that the first 20 minutes are kind of intense. Personally, I think Daniel is Directionally correct on what he lays out in this podcast. I hope that the magnitude of what he is predicting is incorrect on behalf of Life and the things that I care about I have to say though that Since I met him three or four years ago, he's been very accurate on artificial intelligence and what's unfolding in our society with respect to AI. I think we're going to run into energy and other limits before what he suggests in this podcast manifests, but I defer to him on this topic. One final note is we keep this conversation at a high conceptual level, but his team has created a new, 20 plus page, document of references, resources with actual data on what's going on in the ground. We will update that in the show notes on YouTube and on the great simplification.com website, so you can look there, for further data. The conversation continues, my friends. Please welcome Daniel Schmachtenberger."@en ;
    .


