tgs:Interview.TGS_146_BillPlotkin a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bill Plotkin: \"Ecological Awakening: A Path Toward Holistic Adulthood\""@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-24 from a stub, using the full real transcript. Opens with Plotkin reading David Whyte's poem 'All the True Vows' as a deliberate frame-shift before any conceptual content. Central thesis, stated directly: 'the root cause is arrested human development' — Plotkin's own term for what he elsewhere calls 'systemic human development oppression,' offered as the underlying generator behind the metacrisis rather than one crisis among many. Lays out his ecocentric/soulcentric eight-stage developmental wheel (paired earth/human archetypes per stage: Innocent in the Nest, Explorer in the Garden, Thespian at the Oasis, Wanderer in the Cocoon, Apprentice at the Wellspring, Artisan in the Wild Orchard, Master in the Grove of Elders, Sage in the Mountain Cave) — most contemporary people, he argues, never progress past an unhealthy version of the third stage. Describes ecological awakening directly and experientially, not just conceptually: a felt shift from experiencing an 'invisible wall' between oneself and the rest of the living world to experiencing kinship with it, often accompanied by real grief. Closes on Joanna Macy's 'Great Turning' framework (three dimensions: saving what's left, building new structures, shifting consciousness) as Plotkin's own answer to living with the metacrisis, and a direct, unhedged closing claim: the problem is 'worse than you think, and the solution is closer at hand and easier than you might have feared.'"@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.BillPlotkin ;
    dct:created "2024-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-10-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/146-bill-plotkin> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_159_TheRealWork, tgs:Monologue.TGS_205_NateHagens, tgs:Interview.TGS_204_KellyErhart ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/TGS146BillPlotkinTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=S25UKlLq5m8>, <https://open.spotify.com/episode/4u4rGuURrInJOOFPHv5rLu> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.EcologicalAwakening, tgs:Concept.EcocentricDevelopmentalWheel, tgs:Concept.ArrestedHumanDevelopment, tgs:Concept.GreatTurning ;
    dct:references tgs:Persona.RichardSchwartz, tgs:Persona.VanessaAndreotti, tgs:Work.Ishmael, tgs:Work.TheParableOfTheTribes, tgs:Work.HospicingModernity, tgs:Work.WildMind, tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury, tgs:Work.Soulcraft, tgs:Work.TheJourneyOfSoulInitiation, tgs:Work.NatureAndTheHumanSoul, tgs:Work.LookToTheMountains ;
    skos:scopeNote "2026-08-24: built from the full real transcript (previously a bare stub — number/title/dates only, sourced from 3 independent citations across RR-17, Frankly_132, and TGS_167). Real, well-evidenced Persona candidates cited directly in the transcript body (not just show notes) but deliberately NOT bootstrapped this pass, matching this graph's established deferred-citation discipline: Thomas Berry ('The Dream of the Earth' — direct source for the Sage in the Mountain Cave archetype, per Plotkin's own account of meeting him), Riane Eisler (Dominator vs. Partnership societies — real, no TGS episode of hers built yet despite a real cross-reference in this episode's own show notes), Malidoma Somé (Dagara people), Martín Prechtel (Tzutujil Maya), Joanna Macy (already cited, not yet a Persona), and James Hillman ('we've had a hundred years of psychotherapy and the world's getting worse'). Richard Schwartz and Vanessa Andreotti already existed as Personas in this graph — linked via dct:references, not rebuilt. Gregory Cajete and David Whyte are real but cited more lightly (a poem reading; one book title) — noted here rather than flagged as strong candidates."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_147_IndyJohar a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-15"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Today, I'm pleased to be joined by architect and mission steward of Dark Matter Labs, Indy Johar. Indy is also the co-founder of the architecture and urban practice Architecture 00, a founding director of Open Systems Lab, and a non-executive international director of the BloxHub, the Nordic Hub for Sustainable Urbanization. Indy has taught and lectured at various institutions, including Harvard, MIT, and the New School, and is currently a professor at RMIT University. Today, Indy and I have a very wide boundary conversation about how the structure and design of our physical systems in our culture shape our values and behaviors and how they in turn shape the system back. We also dive into some emergent projects and ideas, what he calls fissures, that may be key to leading us towards better human and planetary systems. Perhaps this conversation can plant the seeds of change in viewers like yourself to create your own movements wherever you are towards more pro-social futures. This was a conversation that unexpectedly was both interesting and inspiring to me, and I hope it will be the same for you."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Foundational Challenge: Stewardship, Responsibility, and Designing a New System"@en ;
    skos:definition "First appearance of Indy Johar, architect, co-founder of Dark Matter Labs and Architecture 00 and professor at RMIT University, recorded October 3, 2024. Starting from his phrase 'what we design designs us back', Johar argues that property, contract, employment law and economics shape how we see ourselves, that the value of a house lies mostly in the common goods its land gives access to, and that boundaries in an entangled world create the forcing functions to externalize and extract. He calls the question of what it means to be human now 'the foundational challenge': reimagining ourselves as becomings, multitudes and inter-beings. He describes the institutional 'dark matter' beneath visible crises (insurance withdrawal after Hurricane Helene, not the rain), fissures opening new possibilities (Indigenous and common-law worldviews in Canada, resource-backed currencies in Africa, carbon budgets that allow Europe only about 176,000 new homes a year), 'preemptive peace' for places with planetary-scale risks such as the Amazon and the Himalayas, a re-agentified world and life-ennobling economics, custodianship rather than ownership of materials, and the 'freedom to be radically human'. His sketch of a lower-throughput society starts with food, then e-bikes, spatial justice and a biomaterial economy, and his magic wand would make English a verb-oriented language."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-10-03"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-10-23"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.IndyJohar ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/147-indy-johar> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/TGS147IndyJoharTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_U93lQL5aWA>, <https://open.spotify.com/episode/41R8zJ83qZhkPKA5MlWSVs> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.WhatWeDesignDesignsUsBack, tgs:Concept.InstitutionalDarkMatter, tgs:Concept.PreemptivePeace,
        tgs:Concept.ReAgentificationOfTheWorld, tgs:Concept.LifeEnnoblingEconomics, tgs:Concept.CustodianshipOverOwnership,
        tgs:Concept.FreedomToBeRadicallyHuman, tgs:Concept.FutureOfBeingHuman, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis,
        tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness, tgs:Concept.RightsOfNature, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_127_EdConway, tgs:Interview.TGS_126_AVisionForBetterment, tgs:Interview.TGS_132_WideBoundaryImpactsOfAI,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_71_DanielSchmachtenberger, tgs:Series.BendNotBreak, tgs:Work.TheCodeOfCapital, tgs:Work.Novacene,
        tgs:Organization.DarkMatterLabs ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_238_IndyJohar ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate met Johar a year or two earlier at an Emerge conference in Austin. Johar's journey: a professor's question in his third year of architecture school, 'what's the future of the city?', led him to democratizing the making of places -- WikiHouse (open-source housing), Opendesk (open-source furniture), Impact Hubs, social investment structures. He found institutional logics behind what they made: most of a house's value is land, and land's value comes from monopolistic access to common goods such as labor markets, culture and transport, so property privatizes the commons; in an entangled world, boundaries create the capacity and forcing functions to externalize and extract. Architecture, he says, fuses knowledges and is propositional rather than analytical, and the coding of capital (Katharina Pistor) is rooted in theories of property. 'What we design designs us back' (after Churchill) extends from buildings to contracts and employment law: reduced to incentivized agents we see ourselves as homo economicus; studying economics changes behavior; concentrated power breeds psychopathic asymmetry; 400 years of dominion, perspective, classification and property built our institutions and our self-images, as racialization did despite Africa's greater genetic diversity (see tgs:Concept.WhatWeDesignDesignsUsBack). Nate traces it back to the agricultural revolution, turbocharged by fossil energy. Asked what it means to be human, Johar calls it 'the foundational challenge': moving from individual objecthood to becomings, multitudes and inter-beings (our lungs in relationship with trees), a revolution in how we imagine ourselves (see tgs:Concept.FutureOfBeingHuman); he cites James Lovelock's Novacene on the Earth spending stored solar energy to give birth to a self-aware planetary consciousness, which Nate calls a more poetic version of his energy blindness story. On Marvin Harris's infrastructure and Nate's freeze-the-world thought experiment, Johar says we are locked into 'vector' path dependencies of metrics like GDP and of accumulation, not just static infrastructure. Ways out: fissures (Indigenous and common-law worldviews colliding in Canada, resource-based monies in Africa, Stockholm treating mental health as a collective asset), crises that open the Overton window, 'preemptive peace strikes' for places like the Amazon and the Himalayas whose collapse would be planetary mutually assured destruction (see tgs:Concept.PreemptivePeace), and constraints: within its carbon budget Europe could build about 176,000 homes a year against more than 1.5 million planned. After Asheville's 30 inches of rain, the deeper crisis is insurers withdrawing, which cuts access to mortgages and concentrates assets: the institutional 'dark matter' beneath the visible (see tgs:Concept.InstitutionalDarkMatter). Problems are intersectional, and he praises the podcast's intersectional worldview, citing Ed Conway and Daniel Schmachtenberger. He describes systemic war on the ecological substrate through extraction, felt as 'networked enslavement' in reducing people to units of labor, and offers peace and 'the freedom to be radically human' as a new political language (see tgs:Concept.FreedomToBeRadicallyHuman); sovereignties are exploding beyond the state. Nate maps this to the economic Superorganism, which Johar says is now self-terminating; he proposes a re-agentification of the world -- AI agents, but also rivers and mountains with legal personhood -- with governance shifting from control to learning, and a life-ennobling economics (see tgs:Concept.ReAgentificationOfTheWorld, tgs:Concept.LifeEnnoblingEconomics). Entanglement, from beings to inter-becomings, requires a theory of care, 'power over' giving way to 'being in care with'; he and Nate are in 'a community of fate', and a civilization that cannot transcend competitive evolution may be the answer to the Fermi paradox. Nate notes society shifts to scarcity just as individuals are asked to shift to abundance; Johar calls it a fork, not a gradient, where othering leads toward conflict. COVID's quiet streets gave glimpses of a life beyond homo economicus; care is not a transaction; as machines displace humans as 'bad robots', a new human-machine economy, like the UK's industrial revolution built on prior education reforms, invites a broader theory of being human. On AI (Nate cites his episode with Schmachtenberger), Johar says generative AI is being built into 17th- to 19th-century institutions of control and asset optimization, and its training weights accelerate a dominant, singular perspective; Nate calls it the Walmartization of culture, but Johar also sees a path to re-agentify the world. Education: transition talk ignores development, and we risk handing our traumas to the next generation instead of creating a firebreak; Nate frames two timelines (bend-not-break transition and a different future) and two components (infrastructure and values). Pilots: Jayne Engle's work with the Mi'kmaw Native Friendship Centre on self-sovereign land, city tree canopy strategies (community-planted trees survive far better), custodianship of materials such as electric batteries, and a Taiwanese Indigenous practice of asking the river's permission to borrow stones and promising to return them (see tgs:Concept.CustodianshipOverOwnership). Asked to redesign a lower-throughput society (Nate's 19-terawatt metabolism), Johar starts with food (plant-based diets, rewilded multispecies and local precision farming with controlled-environment buffers), then e-bikes and autonomous three-wheelers, spatial justice for Europe's empty homes held as financial assets, a rebuilt biomaterial economy (the UK has no structural timber forest), demand management, institutions for circularity such as material passports, roof extensions rather than new build, and intangible assets like a city's mental health and decision-making capacity; the city may look much the same while its invisible logics change. On capitalism versus socialism, he says individualism and collectivism share one paradigm of boundaries and othering; cooperatives were 'a brilliant answer to a 19th century problem' but still create the other; money hints at unbounded organizing. The free-rider problem is a 2% problem we turn into a 98% problem by designing every employment contract around fear (Nate: love can be stronger than fear). People who see the crisis recognize that 'any theory of accumulation is a false theory of wealth'; real-world wealth holders need a societal risk hedge. The meme that Earth can carry only a billion people is a delusional crutch for the wealthy, since no smooth throttling exists amid bioweapons and broken supply chains; a lunch-queue conversation can deflate it. Advice: listen to the back catalog, recognize interdependence, keep the extraordinary potential in view, and be conscious of language -- English is object-oriented, many Indigenous languages verb-oriented. To young people: 'this is also bloody extraordinary', a chance to leap the Fermi paradox. He cares most about living with a belief in how extraordinary we can all be, recalling his friend Paul Cardinal; his magic wand would make English a verbing language; for a return visit he proposes 'the next economy of land'."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "2026-10-15: built from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the 2026-09-12 stub (title added earlier the same day from the TGS_238 show notes); the existing back-link from TGS_238 is kept. Host introduction quoted from the auto-generated transcript with names corrected ('Architecture Zero' is Architecture 00, 'Open System Labs' is Open Systems Lab, 'Blocks Hub' is BloxHub); its sharing request is omitted. The teaser line ('freedom to be radically human') is not separately recorded. Garbled words in the transcript: 'Katrina Prestow' is Katharina Pistor; 'Nova Scene' is Novacene; 'homo economists' is homo economicus; 'sub Qur'aqi' (millennia) is unclear; 'Jane Engle' is Jayne Engle; 'mean' (to destroy a meme) is meme; 'common low' is common law; 'fare' is fear; 'piece' is peace in several places; 'Daniel Schmachtenberg' is Schmachtenberger. Spoken details recorded rather than corrected: Nate says Cortes found canals and temples 'over 1000 years ago' (Cortes reached Mexico in 1519); Johar says 8.5 billion people; Europe's ~176,000 homes within its carbon budget versus over 1.5 million planned are per the Dark Matter Labs/Laudes study linked in the show notes. Episode references: Nate's 'episode with Daniel Schmachtenberger on AI and the environment' a few months earlier is TGS_132; the show notes' AI link (youtu.be/_P8PLHvZygo) points to TGS_71, 'Artificial Intelligence and the Superorganism', so both are referenced. In TGS_238 Nate recalls Johar ending this conversation with 'what does it mean to be human'; in this transcript that question comes mid-episode as 'the foundational challenge', and Johar's proposed return topic was 'the next economy of land' (TGS_238 adds that they also spoke by Zoom in between). Not built: Architecture 00, Open Systems Lab, WikiHouse, Opendesk, Impact Hub, BloxHub, Emerge and the Mi'kmaw Native Friendship Centre as Organizations; Jayne Engle, Katharina Pistor, James Lovelock, Marvin Harris and Paul Cardinal as Personas; path dependence, the Overton window, spatial justice and material passports as Concepts."@en ;
    .

