#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_234_SarahWilson a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Today I'm joined by philosopher and writer Sarah Wilson to discuss how to live well through the unfolding more than human predicament, and more specifically, how to remain as engaged, pro-social, and loving humans even as an uncertain world puts pressure on us to act otherwise. Sarah Wilson is a multi New York Times bestselling author and philanthropist. She edited Cosmopolitan Magazine Australia, founded the global I Quit Sugar movement, and hosted MasterChef Australia. She also wrote the bestselling book, First We Make the Beast Beautiful, and her book, This One Wild and Precious Life, won the US Gold Nautilus Literary Prize. Sarah lives nomadically, leading dynamic global conversations about modern philosophy, creativity, existential risk, and climate change via her Wild podcast, as well as her Substack community. Her latest book, I Eat the Stars: How to Live Fully and Beautifully in a Collapsing World, takes us through her journey learning about and then learning to live with the unfolding collapse of complex civilization happening around us. And she and I discuss just that in today's episode, including her journey through letting go of the false hope that if we only act now, we might save the current system we live in, and that letting go of that system might not actually be the worst thing humanity could do in this moment. A small caveat on this episode, I unfortunately had quite a bit of a cold, while recording this conversation, and I was feeling a bit lower in energy. And luckily, Sarah and I are friends, and she brought enough energy and stories to balance our conversation. With that, please welcome Sarah Wilson."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sarah Wilson: Being Thoroughly Human in the Metacrisis"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate and philosopher/author Sarah Wilson (I Eat the Stars) explore the real, emotional arc of confronting the metacrisis directly -- why 'hope,' in its most common form, defers responsibility to a future savior rather than asking anything of us now, and why she grounds herself instead in truth. Real, direct throughline: being 'thoroughly human' (grieving, dancing, showing up for neighbors) as the actual response, not prepping alone, not waiting for politicians or technologists to fix things. Real, honest editorial note carried from the intro: Nate recorded this with a cold and at lower energy than usual, and says so directly."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-09-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.SarahWilson ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/234-sarah-wilson> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/TGS-234-Sarah-Wilson-Transcript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification,
        tgs:Concept.IslandsOfSanity, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_199_SheldonSolomon, tgs:Interview.TGS_167_FrancisWeller,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_146_BillPlotkin, tgs:Interview.TGS_69_RobertLustig, tgs:Interview.TGS_9_PaulEhrlich,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_164_PeterTurchin, tgs:Interview.TGS_217_WisdomInAWorldInCrisis,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_153_LukeKemp, tgs:Interview.TGS_231_GregElliott,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_132_FrameworkForAction, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_157_DecentPeopleIndecentSystem ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct structure: Wilson opens with a real, five-minute 'speed round' definition of the metacrisis -- not just the pile of crises (nuclear, AI, climate, food/water, trade-route disruption) but the crisis OF those crises, the growth-and-individualism mindset underneath all of them, compounded by our own limited consciousness being unable to step outside a system we're implicated in. Real, direct emotional-arc account: Wilson's own path ran through the climate movement specifically -- the real, gradual realization that the 'if we all just rally hard enough' promise wasn't going to hold, which she describes as producing profound RELIEF once she stopped fighting it, not despair, tying this directly to Václav Havel's real, paraphrased distinction between hope-as-optimism and hope-as-things-finally-making-sense. Real, central argument: conventional hope is 'immature,' functionally similar to pessimism, because both defer responsibility -- to a future savior (hope) or to nobody (pessimism). Truth, not hope, is what she grounds herself in now. Real, direct precondition work named throughout, closely paralleling this graph's own Frankly-132 Step 1/Step 2 framing without ever citing it directly: getting one's nervous system 'rock solid and sturdy' (Wheatley's 'islands of sanity'), and the real, explicit argument that intentional/homestead communities under ~100-150 people fail (citing a real study: under 5% last beyond five years) -- so the real answer isn't fleeing to land, it's Pema Chödrön's 'start where you are,' building bonds with people already around you. Real, direct treatment of prepping: rejects solo/violent prepping outright ('are you prepared to defend your tuna and your batteries... with guns and violence?'), endorses 'pro-social prepping' instead -- community-oriented, matching Nate's own real, direct term for the same idea. Real, substantial closing content: duty (not self-flagellating obligation, but Maslow's real, posthumously-discovered tier above self-actualization, and Viktor Frankl's real Auschwitz-derived finding that people with something bigger than themselves survived) as what actually produces lasting meaning, distinct from and outlasting hedonic-treadmill happiness. Real, direct, honest treatment of a hard question: whether to have children now, reframed away from population-growth guilt (peak population projections have moved dramatically closer, 2040-2060 rather than 2080) toward Margaret Mead's real framing that we can't know how a child born into hard times will flourish, and that 70% of trauma survivors experience real post-traumatic growth, not PTSD -- with the London Blitz's own real mental-hospital closures (patients joined the ambulance corps instead) as her worked example. Real, honest limitation named directly, not smoothed over: Wilson is explicit that her own real audience has 'the furthest to fall' and that she has no real, material answer for global-south inequality beyond reducing polarization and inequality generally (citing Peter Turchin's own real two-lever framing) -- she names this as a real boundary of what her own work can address, not a solved problem. Closes on 'be as thoroughly human as you can' -- grieving, dancing, showing up -- framed directly as an act of 'deviance and defiance,' not passive acceptance."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Sebastian Junger (Tribe, post-traumatic growth, directly and repeatedly credited) and Toby Ord (existential risk, the 'humanity as a 16-year-old' framing) are both real, direct, substantial citations without an existing Persona in this graph -- genuine candidates for a future build, not just passing mentions. Margaret Wheatley herself (distinct from her own real 'Islands of Sanity' term, now built as a Concept) is not built as a Persona. Also real, cited-but-not-built: Antonio Gramsci, Milton Friedman, Simone de Beauvoir, Margaret Mead, Viktor Frankl, Pema Chödrön (all historical/literary citations, not TGS guests) -- kept at prose level per this graph's own established discipline for citations that don't carry a direct, real TGS connection. Real, direct connection worth flagging: this episode's own real 'pro-social prepping' term and its close parallel to Frankly-132's own Step 1/Step 2 framing (nervous-system regulation, finding/building with people already around you) are substantively resonant but were never named as such in the transcript -- Wilson and Nate arrive at similar real conclusions independently in conversation, not by directly citing Frankly-132's own framework."@en ;
    .

tgs:Interview.TGS_235_RobertPape a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-10-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Today I'm joined by geopolitical analyst Robert Pape for a deep dive on the unfolding phase shift we are now witnessing in terms of military tactics, strategy, and regional control in the Middle East, as well as the decline of the United States as a global superpower, in coming decades and beyond. Robert A. Pape is professor of political science at the University of Chicago, specializing in international security affairs. His publications include Bombing to Win: Air Power and Coercion in War and Why Economic Sanctions Do Not Work. Additionally, Robert is the director of the Chicago Project on Security and Threats. His current work focuses on how force is used to achieve political objectives, how escalation unfolds, and how strategies of restraint can succeed or fail in international and American political violence. In this spitfire conversation, Robert gives us an update on the Iran war as a result of the recent Ansar Allah attack on the EastWest pipeline in Saudi Arabia. He explains why this is another signal that the conflict is more likely to keep escalating and worsen rather than resolve itself anytime soon. Taking a step further, Robert connects the dots that point toward a larger decline for America's militaristic, diplomatic, and financial power in the world, and what that might mean for the era of globalization and stability that many of us have taken for granted over the last many decades. This was a fast-moving conversation that personally, since I have connected many of these dots, left me with a pit in my stomach about the current state of the world and the near term future and the work that my team at TGS and ISOF are trying to do. That said, Robert's analysis was also illuminating in terms of what we might expect as this war continues on."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Robert Pape: The Escalation Trap"@en ;
    skos:definition "First appearance by University of Chicago political scientist Robert Pape (director, Chicago Project on Security and Threats), recorded September 15, 2026, about six and a half months into the US-Iran war and days after an attack shut Saudi Arabia's East-West pipeline. Pape lays out his escalation trap model, drawn from more than twenty years of modeling US strikes on Iran: tactical success (leaders killed, aim points hit) produces strategic failure as the attacked side gains power, and because Iran's selective control of Hormuz gives it leverage over a fifth of world oil, the attacker cannot simply walk away. He pairs it with the Second Precision Revolution (cheap mass drones plus mines letting weaker states hold off far stronger militaries), argues that Trump's consistent strategy is punishment and that punishment rallies societies rather than breaking them, and traces the consequences: Gulf states caught between Iran and the US, pressure on the petrodollar and Treasury demand, a possible Iranian nuclear test, and the United States on the brink of retreating from global superpower to Western Hemisphere regional power in a more conflict-prone world. His proposed alternative is a multi-year offshore-balancing strategy that builds local militaries rather than escalating or walking away."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-09-15"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-09-23"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.RobertPape ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/235-robert-pape> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_237_ArtBerman ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/TGS-235-Robert-Pape-Transcript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EscalationTrap, tgs:Concept.SecondPrecisionRevolution, tgs:Concept.PunishmentStrategy, tgs:Concept.OffshoreBalancing,
        tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness, tgs:Concept.RelativeFitnessSpiteFramework, tgs:Concept.UltraProcessedInformation ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_61_TheStraitOfHormuzAndTheSpice, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_127_WideBoundaryNews,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_128_UltraProcessedInformation, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_131_IranEffectsLocalPreparednessAndEndOfEmpire,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_133_IranUsAndTheRest, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_135_OilOneOhOne,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_223_MichaelEvery, tgs:Interview.TGS_227_SalMercogliano, tgs:Interview.TGS_229_JeffCurrie,
        tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Work.BombingToWin, tgs:Work.DyingToWin, tgs:Work.OurOwnWorstEnemies,
        tgs:Work.WhyEconomicSanctionsDoNotWork, tgs:Work.HammerAndAnvil, tgs:Work.EscalationTrapSubstack ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate timestamps the conversation (Tuesday, September 15, 2:30 pm) and opens with the news: an attack on Saudi Arabia's East-West pipeline has cut Middle East exports from about 25 million barrels a day to around 13.5, taking the world from roughly 100 million to 88, with the gap coming out of storage. Pape calls it 'fully predictable.' His June 15 Escalation Trap post said Iran would enter a period of maximum leverage from mid-July, open a second Red Sea front, escalate its demands beyond the draft memorandum of understanding to the complete eviction of US forces from the region, and shift from responding to violence to initiating it. Three cushions had softened the loss of Hormuz: strategic petroleum reserves in the US and 31 other countries, China taking demand off the market after its summit with Trump (the quid pro quo still unknown), and the Red Sea bypass through the East-West pipeline to Yanbu. All three were temporary, 'based on twenty years of modeling the bombing of Iran.' Nate's response: 'Holy crap.' On whether Saudi Arabia will even repair the pipeline, Pape compares it to Allied rail bombing in World War II, where tracks were blown up, repaired in weeks and bombed again; pipelines are far more fragile, eight hits did major damage, and a repaired line stays exposed to the Iraqi militias who struck it, the Houthis, Iran itself or ground saboteurs, while the nearby refinery could be hit instead. Nate notes a 'pig in the python' of crude and refined-product shortages is coming even if the war ended today, with Shanghai crude near $135 and US diesel near $6.50, and recalls that three days before the war he said attacking Iran would be 'a Waterloo moment for our president and possibly our country.' Pape defines the escalation trap: stage one, the attacker's bombs hit their aim points and kill the leaders; stage two, the other side does not collapse but gains power; stage three, the attacker cannot walk away. Iran did not need an army to do it: it used the Second Precision Revolution, cheap mass drones combined with mines, to control Hormuz selectively, letting through only its own ships and those that cut deals, which is political control rather than a blunt stoppage and was what General Keane and the White House missed. Iran wants power, not a religious goal, and the US-Israeli attack gave it the pretext. Leaving Vietnam was embarrassing but cost nothing like this, because North Vietnam would never control 20% of the world's energy; walk away from Iran and Iran expands. Nate adds the biophysical frame: about half of the world's recoverable oil lies within roughly 500 miles of Iran, the 'spice' was always its latent power, and the war switched it on. Pape agrees, notes that since the 1970s the US has sought to prevent any oil hegemon in the Gulf, and explains that Iran waited to be attacked in both June 2025 and February 2026 so the blame for a global economic shock would fall on the US and Israel, which polls show it now does, more on America and Trump than on Israel. Worse, he says, the war has ended America's role as protector that began with the 1991 Gulf War, whose quick success underwrote 35 years of globalization in which countries cut military spending and leaned on trade; that is now reversing. Asked about US strategy, Pape separates tactics, which have oscillated, from strategy, which he defines by its mechanism (action, mechanism, outcome): Trump's consistent strategy, with tariffs and at home as well as in Iran, is punishment, imposing pain now with the threat of more, escalating from killing leaders to bombing to an economic blockade from April 12 that punishes the whole society. Punishment provokes fight or flight, not cooperation, and is making the IRGC and the government more popular. He illustrates with Alvin, the sixth-grade bully at his elementary school, whom he finally charged and who never bothered him again, and with the Blitz: German bombing of London and Coventry led the British public to demand payback, Churchill replaced his air staff, and Bomber Harris planned the firebombing of 58 German towns. Nate calls the US approach '5D reverse spite' because it hurts the US most over time. On the Gulf states, Pape says Mohammed bin Salman urged Trump and Netanyahu toward the February 28 regime-change strikes, a story now published in The New York Times, then refused US overflight in April and moved toward neutrality, which will not save him: Iran may work through Saudi tribes and leaders unhappy with the House of Saud, seeking a new government in Riyadh as Putin seeks one in Kyiv, and Pape doubts a deal can erase the killings. He describes the US as having gone from near preeminence to vulnerability in the Gulf, with bases and the Fifth Fleet's Bahrain base heavily damaged, the USS Abraham Lincoln resupplying from Diego Garcia, and carriers threatened by Mach 5 missiles that Aegis cruisers cannot stop. Financially, Iran's aim will be to destabilize the Gulf governments enough that their central banks stop buying, or eventually dump, US Treasuries bought with petrodollars, and to push toward oil traded in a basket of currencies, which Iran's leadership raised at the BRICS summit; the memorandum failed because Washington treated great power politics as a business deal. Iran's diplomatic outreach to Russia, China and India and talk of a 'new security architecture' without the US leave China happy to see America lose. Asked whether this is just who we are, between bonobos and chimps, Pape says his next book will study strong states that launched disastrous preventive wars (Germany before both world wars, Japan in 1941, Russia in 2022, now the US) and suspects inordinate power breeds overconfidence and hubris. Asked about striking the enrichment site at Pickaxe Mountain and walking away, he expects Iran's next step to be one or two nuclear tests, possibly an 'October surprise,' notes reports of officials considering tactical nuclear weapons and a CSIS satellite study he reads as preparing the public, and says using them would break the 80-year taboo and threaten demand at Treasury auctions with US debt at $40 trillion and the 30-year yield near 5.3%; Nate adds that 10-year notes had just hit 5% and that 8 or 9% would kill the economy. His best case, more feasible after the midterms in January, is a multi-year offshore-balancing strategy aimed at regional stability rather than regime change or retreat, with the US doing about 20% of the heavy lifting: the model that beat ISIS with air power supporting a ground coalition including Syrian Kurds, and that let Ukraine hold off Russia because US advisers spent years after 2014 building a professional officer and NCO corps and logistics, not because of money or Javelins. Gulf states could run the tanker interdiction themselves with drones. Without a change, the US is on the brink of shrinking to a Western Hemisphere regional power; Europe now asks not whether America has the will to support Ukraine but whether it has the capability, South Korea and Japan likely ask the same, and power vacuums will make a far more conflict-prone world: 'we're gonna miss the days before this war.' His closing advice is to use social media for depth rather than team A versus team B, because people are hungry for depth."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "SHOW-NOTES CORRECTIONS: the show notes carry asterisked fact-checks, and as with TGS_237 the sourced figure is treated as authoritative where it differs from what was said aloud. The pipeline attack was attributed to Iran-backed militias in Iraq rather than the Houthis (Nate opened by crediting Ansar Allah; Pape later also names the Iraqi militia). The Abqaiq facility is in Saudi Arabia's Eastern Province, not near Yanbu (Pape: 'a giant refinery near Yanbu called Abqaiq'). China's crude imports fell 3.9 million barrels a day (Pape: five million). Hormuz carries about 20% of world oil and the East-West pipeline 4 to 5 million barrels a day, about 5%. Shanghai crude futures hit a record $138.50 and US retail diesel was $6.29 (Nate: $135 and $6.50). The Persian Gulf holds about 55% of world oil reserves (Pape: over half within roughly 150 miles). Desert Storm took 43 days with 148 US battle deaths (Pape: 147), and the Pentagon's worldwide body-bag stockpile was 63,000 with the Gulf allocation classified (Pape: ten thousand taken). The House of Saud numbers about 15,000, with about 2,000 holding most power and wealth (Pape: four thousand princes). The US had 19 military sites in the region, 8 permanent, with 40,000 to 50,000 troops (Pape: thirteen bases, fifty thousand troops). The hammer-and-anvil campaign against ISIS ran three years across three phases (Pape: four solid years). Gulf crude exports were at about two-thirds of pre-war levels and global inventories were drawing down 3.8 million barrels a day. NOT CORRECTED IN THE SHOW NOTES, recorded here: Pape calls Araghchi 'the president of Iran'; the show notes link the name to Abbas Araghchi, who has served as Iran's foreign minister. Pape refers on air to his June 15 post as 'Iran and the era of maximum leverage'; the linked post is titled 'Uncertain Deal, And Iran Certainly Enters Its Period of Maximum Leverage.' No YouTube replay URL was in the show notes. The canonical URL follows the site's {number}-{guest} pattern and was already used, as a raw link, by TGS_237 before this build. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: General Jack Keane, Herman Kahn (escalation ladder, show notes only), Arthur 'Bomber' Harris, and the Chicago Project on Security and Threats as an Organization."@en ;
    .

tgs:Interview.TGS_236_ZekeHausfather a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-10-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Today I'm joined by Zeke Hausfather, a climate scientist and systems analyst, for a deep dive into what the various numbers and models and acronyms in climate science are actually telling us about our rapidly changing biosphere. Zeke is a lead author of the IPCC Seventh Assessment report. He also has authored the climate trends chapter of the Fifth US National Climate Assessment, and has testified as an expert witness before the US House Committee on Science, Space and Technology. At Berkeley Earth, Zeke helps produce one of the world's primary global surface temperature records. He is also a climate science contributor at Carbon Brief, as well as the lead climate researcher at Frontier. In this conversation, Zeke shares where we're currently at compared to the prior IPCC models, what that actually means, and how the IPCC is updating its models for the upcoming seventh report in order to understand the biggest climate questions of recent years, including, is global heating accelerating? Beyond that, we also discuss what the current Super El Nino means for warming in twenty twenty-seven and beyond, as well as Zeke's recent analysis on the surprising energy requirements of using AI and large language models for environmental research. If you enjoy digging into the weeds of climate science and getting a better understanding of what climate research is based on and how reliable it is, this one is for you."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Zeke Hausfather: Reading the Numbers of Climate Science"@en ;
    skos:definition "First appearance by climate scientist Zeke Hausfather (Berkeley Earth; lead author of Chapter 2 of the IPCC Seventh Assessment Report; Carbon Brief; climate research lead for Stripe's Frontier carbon-removal fund), recorded September 16, 2026 as a record El Niño was building. A tour of what climate science's numbers mean: how global temperature is measured and why the 14 independent records agree on about 1.4°C of warming; how the IPCC works; the emissions scenarios from IS92 to the new CMIP7 set (now named high, medium, low and very low); forcings versus feedbacks; climate sensitivity; why warming roughly stops, but does not reverse, at zero emissions; why 2027, not 2026, will be the record year at about 1.8°C; aerosol unmasking as the main driver of recent acceleration; Earth's energy imbalance and cloud and methane feedbacks; the energy use of AI agents; and his estimates of about 3°C by 2100 if emissions stay flat (range roughly 2 to 4°C). Nate pushes back that cheap solar has been added on top of fossil fuels rather than replacing them; Hausfather argues for treating climate as a normal, solvable problem, warns against doom, and closes on carbon pricing and carbon removal research."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-09-30"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ZekeHausfather ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/236-zeke-hausfather> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/TGS-236-Zeke-Hausfather-Transcript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.ClimateSensitivity, tgs:Concept.AerosolMasking, tgs:Concept.ZeroEmissionsCommitment, tgs:Concept.EarthEnergyImbalance,
        tgs:Concept.ClimateAsANormalProblem, tgs:Concept.ScenarioThinking, tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox, tgs:Concept.Rebuildables,
        tgs:Concept.EnergyAdditionsNotTransitions, tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_105_LeonSimons, tgs:Interview.TGS_134_JohanRockstrom, tgs:Interview.TGS_219_TadPatzek,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_61_TheStraitOfHormuzAndTheSpice, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_72_WhatIfClimateChangeWasAHoax,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_138_Part1ChangingTheFuture, tgs:Series.HowToThinkAboutTheFuture,
        tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Work.TheClimateBrink, tgs:Work.TheRealEnergyUseOfAgenticAI,
        tgs:Work.CMIP7ScenariosForAR7, tgs:Work.RecentStrengtheningOfEasternPacificENSO ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate notes Hausfather's unusual path: he founded a clean-tech company in grad school and ran it for about a decade before his PhD, and now works across Berkeley Earth, Stripe and Frontier, The Climate Brink, Carbon Brief and the IPCC. What that taught him: the real world is far messier than academic models, and in his energy-efficiency company the only lasting behavior changes were 'set it and forget it' ones (LED bulbs, efficient appliances, EVs), so he trusts solutions that improve people's lives more than those demanding sacrifice; Nate suggests neighborhood-wide norms as the untested alternative, and Hausfather agrees social pressure works but is hard to engineer. On measuring temperature: ship-bucket sea measurements were biased by deck height, Antarctica had almost no stations until mid-century, groups choose how to fill gaps (for example kriging), yet the 14 independent records worldwide agree closely over the last 50 years on about 1.4°C of warming; for AR7 a community paper led by Peter Thorne proposes a 10,000-member ensemble in place of 14 separate lines. He describes the IPCC as a 'well-oiled machine,' policy-relevant but not prescriptive, whose international collaboration makes him more optimistic in a polarized world, and explains its three working groups (physical science; impacts; mitigation), with adaptation growing in both II and III. Asked by Nate, who has just run a Frankly scenario series, to explain the RCPs and SSPs, he says about half the uncertainty in future warming is human choices, which physics cannot answer, hence scenarios: IS92 (1992), SRES (2000), RCPs (early 2010s), SSPs, and now the CMIP7 scenarios released a few months earlier, which extend to 2150 and replace numeric names (radiative forcing in watts per square meter in 2100) with high, medium, low and very low. He separates external forcings from feedbacks and walks through carbon-cycle feedbacks (ocean acidification limiting uptake, CO2 fertilization versus fire, drought and drying soils); for AR7, Earth system models without a dynamic carbon cycle are excluded from the main projections. Climate feedbacks (clouds, water vapor, snow and ice albedo) produce the climate-sensitivity range of 2 to 5°C per CO2 doubling, a 90% range with more risk on the high side; he corrects Nate's timescales (transient response is about 20 years, equilibrium sensitivity 300 to 500 years, Earth system sensitivity longer) and explains that these are idealized fixed-concentration experiments. If emissions actually reach zero, falling CO2 and a still-warming ocean roughly cancel, so warming stops but the Earth does not cool back down without removing carbon. Nate asks whether this year will be the warmest of our lives and the coolest of the rest; Hausfather flips it: El Niño's global effect lags its peak by three to five months, as in 1997-98, 2015-16 and 2023-24, so 2027 will be 'stunning,' about 1.8°C against a previous record of 1.5°C. A strong El Niño is a preview of the average a decade later: El Niños add about 0.2°C the following year, and human emissions add about 0.2°C every eight years, 'a permanent super El Niño worth of heat... every eight years.' He describes the five SSPs and why SSP3, a poor, nationalist, conflict-ridden world with weak cooperation and low adaptive capacity, feels close to home; the new high scenario is built on it, with emissions about 50% higher by 2100. Nate says 'zero chance of that'; Hausfather notes fossil use rose 30% in the decade 2000-2010. He argues the old RCP8.5-type high ends assumed cheap coal and expensive clean energy and now look implausible because solar is cheap even in a poor world. Nate disagrees: the same coal engine builds the 'rebuildable' panels and turbines, and from 2015 to 2025 fossil fuels grew far more than solar, so cheaper solar does not help the climate unless the economic system, values and governance change. Hausfather concedes it will not solve the problem alone but says coal has roughly plateaued since 2015, about 30% of cars sold globally this year are electric (5% in the US), wind and solar will out-generate gas globally for the first time this year, China's emissions are flat to falling, the US and Europe are down about 20% and the UK 40% with no coal, yet flat global emissions still lead to close to 3°C, which would be 'pretty catastrophic.' CO2 is just under 430 ppm and rising linearly while emissions are flat; it would start to fall once emissions are roughly halved, and at zero might drop about 80 ppm. The ocean is 'our friend' because water's huge heat capacity has absorbed 90% of trapped heat (ocean surface up about 1°C, land about 2°C, the deep ocean barely), but it will work against any effort to cool the planet. On the record El Niño: Niño 3.4 is already over 3°C above normal in mid-September and may peak near 4°C, more than a degree above the 1877-78 and 2015-16 events; the 1877-78 El Niño caused famines killing an estimated 50 million, mainly through drought around the tropical Pacific (southern Africa, India, Indonesia, northern Brazil, Australia). Nate adds forecasts of caloric shortages and regional famines in 2028 from Hormuz fertilizer shortfalls; Hausfather notes the WMO's largest-ever mobilization and preparations in Kenya and southern Africa, against deep aid cuts, with USAID 'fed through the wood chipper.' He cautions that El Niño is mainly natural variability, though a new thousand-year Galápagos coral record finds recent El Niños unusually strong, and that warming itself has accelerated mainly because aerosol pollution, which masked about a third of historical warming, has been cut while CO2 keeps rising. Climate concern is 'thermostatic' and a 'political luxury good,' rising when life feels secure, so 2027 may refocus attention. On aerosols, following Nate's earlier episode with Leon Simons: sulfur dioxide cools directly and by seeding low clouds; masking may be anywhere from 0.2 to 1.2°C, and high-sensitivity models need high masking; global SO2 is down 40%, China's 75%, shipping's about 80% after the 2020 IMO rule; in his estimate nearly all the recent acceleration, from about 0.2 to about 0.3°C per decade, comes from these cuts, but with millions dying yearly from air pollution he would 'much rather take the climate warming' and clean the air. Earth's energy imbalance, which Tad Patzek told Nate is about 20 times the human economy, is measured directly by the CERES satellites and rising faster; whether the extra absorbed energy reflects aerosol cuts or a temperature-driven loss of low clouds is a central open question, with high-sensitivity models showing the larger cloud loss. Methane caused about 0.4°C of warming but breaks down within about a decade, making it a fast lever for cooling; permafrost methane is still regionally concerning but globally minor, while tropical wetlands, possibly a temperature feedback, drive much of the recent rise. Nate compares Hausfather's answers to an AI's and asks about AI in climate science: machine learning struggles with physics, though AI weather models are excellent, and AI may 'recenter expertise' as a consensus machine in a fragmented information ecosystem. His own study of four months of his Claude Code use found a session uses about 0.2 to 1.2 kWh and a day of agent use 1.2 to 6 kWh, about two refrigerators, compared with 0.24 Wh for a Gemini prompt or 0.34 Wh for ChatGPT, and he expects Jevons paradox (as with LED lighting) to push AI energy use up; Nate agrees it applies to solar too. His central range for 2100 with flat emissions is about 3°C (roughly 2 to 4, with the low end of models near 2.2), so planning should target the tails rather than the median; with policy rollback, about 3.5°C (2.5 to 4.5), with perhaps a one-in-four chance of 5°C by 2150. Nate argues ethically that 2500 matters as much as 2100. Forests matter as a sink: land-use emissions are about a tenth of the total, and the biosphere provides a little under half of the net sink, which Nate fears fuel shortages could erode, as when Greece guarded forests from people cutting wood for heat. On US withdrawal from the IPCC and cuts to climate.gov, the disasters database and a long sea-ice record, data aggregation can be replaced by Europe's Copernicus, but lapsed satellite measurements leave gaps that can never be filled; explaining to Chinese colleagues that his president calls the field a Chinese hoax is 'a little embarrassing.' On alarm fatigue, he quotes Stephen Schneider that the end of the world and 'good for us' are the two least likely outcomes, argues for treating climate as a normal problem as with 'AI as a normal technology,' and blames politics that only responds to crises. His advice: vote and contact representatives, and adopt solar, EVs and heat pumps early to drive costs down and make policy cheaper; for young people, stay engaged, accept that 1.5°C is gone but a manageable outcome is not, and avoid doom, since 'apathy... is our worst enemy.' He cares most about his two small children and about functional politics, missing the 1990s-2000s when parties could still cooperate. His magic-wand answer is 'the boring economist answer': internalize social costs by pricing carbon and habitat loss. Finally he describes Frontier's carbon removal work, about 10% of the solution against 90% from cutting emissions: enhanced rock weathering on acidic croplands (speeding the geological silicate-weathering thermostat), river liming with Canada's Carbon Run, ocean alkalinity enhancement, biomass approaches and direct air capture, with the aim of learning this decade what can scale. Less than 1% of the $2.3 trillion spent on emissions reductions goes to removal; Nate counters that the $2.3 trillion has not cut global emissions, and Hausfather agrees only relative to what would otherwise have happened."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "SHOW-NOTES CORRECTIONS: the show notes carry asterisked fact-checks, treated as authoritative where they differ from what was said aloud (as with TGS_235 and TGS_237). There was no substantial Antarctic station network before the 1957-58 International Geophysical Year (Hausfather: nothing until 1950). Transient climate response is the response at CO2 doubling averaged over 20 years centered on doubling. His 2027 forecast is about 1.71°C above pre-industrial against a 1.5°C record (on air and in the show-notes description: about 1.8°C). The CMIP7 high scenario reaches 55 gigatons of CO2 a year in 2100. Global fossil CO2 emissions rose 31% from 2001 to 2010, from 25 to 33 gigatons (Hausfather: 30% more fossil fuels burned 2000-2010). Global solar generation rose from 256 TWh in 2015 to 2,778 TWh in 2025, an increase of about 2,500 TWh (Nate: 4,400 TWh). In an idealized experiment, atmospheric CO2 fell a median 80 ppm 50 years after emissions ceased. NOAA CPC reported a weekly Niño 3.4 anomaly of 2.0°C on 14 September 2026, with previous record ONI peaks of 2.6°C (2015-16) and 2.4°C (1997-98) (Hausfather: above 3°C now, previous record about 2.75°C; the two may use different indices). The thousand-year Galápagos coral paper was published in Science, not Nature as said on air. Aerosols cool the planet by about 0.50°C, range 0.22-0.96°C (Hausfather: 0.2 to 1.2°C). Global SO2 emissions are down 40% since the mid-2000s (Hausfather: since a peak around 1980); China's are down 75% since 2007 (Hausfather: since 2008). Shipping was 4-9% of anthropogenic SO2 before IMO 2020 (Hausfather: about 10%). WHO counts 4.2 million premature deaths a year from ambient air pollution, 6.7 million including household (Hausfather: five to eight million from outdoor). Land-use CO2 emissions averaged 1.4 gigatons of carbon a year over 2015-2024 (Hausfather: about four gigatons of carbon, about 10% of emissions). The ended record is a 47-year passive-microwave sea-ice dataset (Nate: 40-year). NOT CORRECTED IN THE SHOW NOTES, recorded here: Hausfather says '2023 was considerably warmer than it' where he means 2024; the show notes link the Tad Patzek energy-imbalance claim to TGS_219, though the more recent Patzek episode Nate may mean is TGS_225 (June 2026, on the super El Niño), not verified, so only TGS_219 is referenced. The show notes' 'Nate's Frankly series on scenario planning' link is the How to Think About the Future playlist, modeled here as a reference to tgs:Series.HowToThinkAboutTheFuture. No YouTube replay URL was in the show notes. The canonical URL follows the site's {number}-{guest} pattern, not independently confirmed. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Peter Thorne, Stephen Schneider, Nan Ransohoff, Berkeley Earth, Carbon Brief, Stripe/Frontier, Carbon Run and the IPCC as Organizations; enhanced rock weathering and carbon dioxide removal as concepts."@en ;
    .

tgs:Interview.TGS_237_ArtBerman a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-10-07"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-07"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Today I'm joined by longtime TGS guest Art Berman for a wide boundary update on the geopolitical and energy situation surrounding the Middle East and the Strait of Hormuz, looking beyond the chaos of the day-to-day events towards the longer term impacts for the supply and circulation of oil, gas, and other critical materials. Art Berman is a petroleum geologist with over 40 years of oil and gas industry experience. He is an expert on US shale plays and is currently consulting for many E&P companies and capital groups in the energy sector. In this conversation, Art and I dig into the buffers that have kept some parts of the system afloat, even while others have already begun to feel the pain of expensive oil and natural gas prices. More importantly, Art explains that while these buffers prevented some of the most immediate consequences that many analysts warned about, the resilience in the system is now quickly wearing thin, leaving us on the precipice of the, largest energy shock we've ever seen, with almost no slack left, unless things change. All the while, the power lines of geopolitics are rearranging as countries realign themselves with the materials and resources and protection they'll need amidst an uncertain and rapidly changing world. These are interesting but serious times that we live in, and no one human can carry it all. I appreciate each of you continuing to tune in, and learn what's happening in our world alongside me and my guests. With that, please welcome Art Berman."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "A World-Changing Event: We're Not Going Back to January 2026"@en ;
    skos:definition "Return appearance by petroleum geologist Art Berman, a follow-up to his May 2026 conversation (TGS-220), recorded roughly four months into the Iran war's disruption of Gulf energy flows. Berman's central argument: the system proved far more adaptable than he, and most analysts he respects, expected -- the world lost roughly 10 million barrels a day rather than 20 -- but only because of buffers (bypass pipelines, strategic reserves, an unexplained fall in Chinese crude imports) that are now largely spent, and the adaptations carry real costs that headlines omit. Berman and Nate trace the parallels and differences with the 1970s-80s oil shocks (see tgs:Concept.EnergyGrowthRateDependence), argue there has never been an energy transition, only additions (see tgs:Concept.EnergyAdditionsNotTransitions), and ask whether a fragmenting, multipolar world of resource nationalism is replacing the postwar security architecture that underwrote cheap oil. Berman's bottom line: 'we are not going back to January 2026.'"@en ;
    dct:created "2026-09-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-10-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ArtBerman ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/237-art-berman> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/TGS-237-Art-Berman-Transcript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cWCGyrZWRTY&t=1s> ;
     thinkr:discusses 
        tgs:Concept.PeakOil, 
        tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness, 
        tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, 
        tgs:Concept.Superorganism,
        tgs:Concept.BiophysicalGauntlet, 
        tgs:Concept.ComplexityCollapse,
        tgs:Concept.EnergyAdditionsNotTransitions, 
        tgs:Concept.EnergyGrowthRateDependence ;
    dct:references 
        tgs:Interview.TGS_101_ShaleOilAndTheSlurpingSound, 
        tgs:Interview.TGS_220_AWorldOnThePrecipice,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_229_JeffCurrie, 
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_3_EnergyBlindness,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_61_TheStraitOfHormuzAndTheSpice, 
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_65_AndThenWhat,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes, 
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_124_WideBoundaryNews,
        tgs:Work.EnergyTransitionsOrAdditions, 
        tgs:Work.RealityBlind,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_235_RobertPape,
        <https://www.artberman.com/blog/the-iran-war-phase-shift-energy-shock-rising-complexity-and-the-end-of-the-old-growth-regime/>,
        <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Art-Berman-Presentation-Iran-Podcast-SEP-2026.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Opening thesis: the war has 'ripped the Band-Aid off' a system that depends on an ever-rising rate of energy growth even though that rate has been falling since the late 1960s (see tgs:Concept.EnergyGrowthRateDependence); removing about 10 million barrels a day almost overnight shows how unprepared the world is, and the energy needed just to maintain the system at 8.2 billion people is enormous and rarely appreciated. Why it wasn't worse: the US has a large endowment of oil, gas, and coal, while Asia, Africa, and (Nate adds) Europe lack indigenous supply and are suffering; and the system adapted far better than Art expected in May -- he says he got diesel and product prices right but was wrong that Hormuz would be an immediate heart attack, as were most analysts he respects. Instead of losing 20 million barrels a day the world lost about 10, thanks to bypass pipelines, strategic reserve draws, and a puzzling drop in Chinese crude imports; 'a trip to the drugstore for Tylenol' rather than the emergency room -- but, Art says, 'we're getting the car warmed up right now.' Buffers: the US Strategic Petroleum Reserve is at multi-decade lows, EIA pulled its SPR data a few weeks earlier, and nobody, including the Department of Energy, knows the level at which the salt caverns become damaged; Art stresses that rates matter more than amounts, and the SPR drawdown rate has fallen from millions of barrels a day to a couple of hundred thousand a week, which he calls not good news. He thinks the buffers were effectively exhausted weeks ago, with a drone attack on Saudi Arabia's East-West pipeline (4-5 million barrels a day) tying a bow on it. Nate's wide-boundary reframe: Middle East reserves are themselves civilization's buffer of high-quality liquid hydrocarbons, and peak oil may turn out to be driven more by above-ground factors (alliances, security architecture, access) than by geological depletion. Stocks versus flows: Art says 14 million barrels a day of Gulf production is shut in, which has never happened before -- an n-equals-one experiment. History: the 1979-81 shocks were comparable as a share of supply and their effects lasted about 15 years, but recovery then was easier because high prices triggered a deepwater exploration boom, the Soviet collapse opened new supply, reserves were far larger, and fossil fuels were the only game in town; today, after 20 years of 'twilight industry' messaging (ESG, divestment, peak-oil-demand narratives), nobody wants to fund deepwater, Arctic, or new refineries, and Dangote in Nigeria is the only major new refinery Art knows of. Demand growth has slowed from about 2 to about 1 million barrels a day per year while maintaining the system now takes more than supplying it, growth included, did 40-50 years ago; asked whether the war widened or narrowed Nate's biophysical gauntlet (see tgs:Concept.BiophysicalGauntlet), Art says he doesn't know, but notes the 1980s energy system was globalized while today's is fragmented, with China and Russia reorganizing energy and currency flows around their own preferences. Adaptation is not free: ship-to-ship Hormuz shuttles cost time, money, and risk; the East-West pipeline delivers to the Red Sea, where Houthi attacks force rerouting through Suez into the Mediterranean -- the Atlantic Basin, not Asia -- leaving 'the whole oil system in the wrong place at the wrong time'; the headline says 'more barrels out of the Gulf,' the article says more friction and oil not getting where it's needed. Nate calls political messaging Orwellian (the claim the war ends after the midterms and gasoline drops 'like a stone,' which he says would itself signal deep recession) and argues current prices are the new floors, not ceilings. Even if peace broke out tomorrow: months before shipping normalizes (mines, war-risk insurance, a tanker struck a mine the week before), and the real concern is production -- shut-in wells are a complex plumbing system of microscopic rock pore spaces, and Art's experience-based guess is that roughly 5 to 15 or 20 percent of shut-in production will never return to prior levels, with the rest taking weeks to months. The 1980s price path (about $145 a barrel, then about $45 for the rest of the decade and the 1990s) reflected a long developing-world recession. Refined products: nobody owns a barrel of crude except refineries; diesel is above $200 a barrel equivalent (Nate cites about $240 in China); refining capacity was already constrained by under-investment; electric vehicles still depend on oil for plastics and asphalt; Art calls running the global economy mainly on non-fossil sources 'a fantasy' for now -- one could run an economy that way, but not for 8.2 billion people. Energy additions, not transitions (see tgs:Concept.EnergyAdditionsNotTransitions): there has never been an energy transition, only layering of new sources over old, and the investment world's substitution mindset has no historical precedent. 1970s responses (nuclear build-out, efficiency standards, North Sea oil) versus today's options (faster electrification, a return to coal including China's expected 2027 coal expansion, resource nationalism); Art expects a price run-up, demand destruction varying by place, and eventually a version of the post-1980 stagnation -- 'not gonna play like that movie did' because today's scale and complexity are far greater. US shale is 'no longer viewed as having the potential to make a difference'; Nate's list of strains (10-year yields near 5%, $40 trillion debt, no new refineries, shale peaked outside the Permian, a deteriorating relationship with the Middle East 'fossil bank account,' a waning security architecture, renewables adding rather than replacing) set against narrow-boundary optimism (AI boom, record stock markets) and the Titanic's band still playing. Art calls the technology story a mythology in the literal sense -- a magical, and genuinely impressive, story -- but one inseparable from ever-growing energy supply; the Green Revolution is the textbook rebuttal of Malthus, yet came with energy costs and externalities (fertilizer pollution, falling water tables, soil salinization), and every technology has energy, ecological, and human costs with diminishing returns. Beyond oil: natural gas is every bit as urgent -- protests in Syria, Bangladesh, and Pakistan over LNG-dependent power prices, Europe paying roughly $25-27 per million BTU against under $3 in the US, Volkswagen closing plants, Europe having swapped Russian gas for pricey US and Qatari LNG; Nate raises the fertilizer chokepoint (urea and ammonia through Hormuz), reports of a 2027-28 caloric shortfall, a European drought, a coming Super El Niño, and Ukraine's blocked ports. Buffers and game theory: Art cites Jeff Currie (TGS-229) on state capitalism, which Nate equates with resource nationalism, seeing hints in US government stakes such as a Venezuelan venture -- which Art says is mostly talking points, since Venezuelan heavy crude cannot make gasoline and the lag exceeds consumers' patience; US light oil is superb for gasoline ('if the world ran on gasoline the US would be king') but the US lacks the heavy components for diesel, importing about 4 million barrels a day from Canada while picking fights with its most important supplier, and upgrading heavy crude through hydrogen-intensive refinery processes. Security architecture (a question Nate says he also put to Robert Pape, Ep 235): the global trade and security order rests on the US guaranteeing free maritime trade in exchange for reserve-currency status, an implicit bargain; Art says the US has 'abjectly failed' at that role in this war, everybody knows it, and Gulf states are quietly pursuing other security partners; the petrodollar story is 'iffier' -- Kissinger's 1974 Saudi arrangement has arguably not existed for 20 years, though the Eurodollar credit system remains a near-American monopoly now facing new uncertainty. The deeper message: far more intense, open competition for remaining critical resources, with China and Russia 'way ahead' in anticipating it, the Trump administration's critical-minerals push as catch-up, and World Wars I and II 'won and lost on the basis of availability of oil.' What would change Art's mind: authentic US-Iran talks (as a first data point), countries rebuilding strategic reserves, more oil flowing from Hormuz and the Red Sea without coercive security arrangements, and above all shut-in production actually restarting and the additional barrels IEA and EIA project -- EIA sees about 112 million barrels a day by late 2027, which Art rates very low probability; Nate adds that agency forecasts carry an optimism bias, and Art notes their data remains valuable. Closing advice: don't get distracted by narrow-boundary details like whether 7 or 9 million barrels a day exit Hormuz -- watch how much shut-in production returns, how the world responds to high diesel and jet fuel prices, and the likely demand destruction behind China's import cut; however it turns out, 'we're not going back to the way things were not very long ago.' Nate closes with plans to talk again in about six months."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "the IRI follows the guest-name convention (as with TGS_229/233/234) so it will not need to change. The canonical URL is inferred from the site's consistent {number}-{guest} slug pattern (92-, 101-, 220-art-berman; 229-jeff-currie; 235-robert-pape), not independently confirmed. SHOW-NOTES CORRECTIONS: the show notes carry asterisked fact-checks; where a figure Art states aloud differs from the sourced figure, the show notes' figure is treated as authoritative (same editorial-precedence logic used for Frankly transcripts versus their later Substack essays): fossil fuels were 86% of global energy in 2025 (Art: about 80%); China's seaborne crude imports fell 3.6 million barrels a day February-April (Art: 4 to 4.5); SPR drawdowns peaked in May (Art: June); Gulf output was 14.4 million barrels a day below pre-war in the May IEA report with more than 10 still shut in per the September report (Art's 14 is the May-vintage figure); European gas near $27 per million BTU (Art: about $25); imported crude near $148 in early 1981 in today's dollars (Art: about $145); traditional biomass use roughly double its 1800 level (Art: about the same as 1800). One outright factual correction: Art says LNG cannot be transferred ship-to-ship because it is super-cooled; the show notes correct this -- LNG is routinely transferred ship-to-ship, and one operator has completed more than 3,000 such transfers. Art's shut-in-production loss estimate is his own experience-based guess, offered as such (the show notes' description rounds it to 5 to 20 percent). The transcript is auto-generated and not fully proofed. GAPS: Robert Pape's TGS-235 is referenced by URL only -- it, and TGS-236, are not in this graph, and Pape has no Human or Persona entry. Not built: Chris Wright (US Energy Secretary, whose claim of record Gulf flows Art treats skeptically), Henry Kissinger. Resource nationalism / state capitalism gets only a brief exchange here and is deliberately not minted as a Concept now; it is a candidate for when TGS-229 (Jeff Currie) is built. Nate's narrow- versus wide-boundary framing is invoked explicitly (the show notes link Frankly 65), but the graph has no foundational wide-boundary Concept to link."@en ;
    .

tgs:Interview.TGS_238_IndyJohar a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-10-15"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-15"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Today, I'm very pleased to welcome back Indy Johar to the program for an exploration on how we might rebuild and adapt our land, our institutions, and our capital markets to expand the number of better possible futures for humanity, or as he calls it, civilizational optionality. Indy Johar is an architect and co-founder of Project 00. He is also a professor at RMIT University, as well as a non-executive director of WikiHouse Foundation and Bloxhub. Most recently, Indy founded Dark Matter Labs, a field laboratory focused on building the institutional infrastructure for radical civic societies, cities, regions, and towns. This was one of the best conversations I have had on this platform. It was in New York City during Climate Week. I was exhausted, going into this interview, but something about Indy's energy and his ideas pulled the best out of me, and perhaps that created some two-way synergy. We talked about the importance of financial markets seeing the dead-end aspects of our current path, and about humanity becoming self-aware and about our moment in history. When I talk about emergence, this conversation is an example of that. I do expect it's, for some, gonna be pretty academic and fancy concepts, but the implications of what we talked about here, in my opinion, are one of the few pathways forward for humanity and the biosphere."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Civilizational Optionality: How Coupled Systemic Risks Are Reshaping Capital, Institutions, and the Future of Being Human"@en ;
    skos:definition "Return appearance by Indy Johar, architect and co-founder of Dark Matter Labs and Project 00, recorded in New York City during Climate Week, about two years after TGS_147. Johar argues that civilization's institutions and most accumulated wealth were built on the predictability of the Holocene, of energy supply and of demographics, and that this predictability is ending as climate breakdown, nutrition and fertilizer shocks, resource conflict and the disruption of humans as units of labor and cognition begin to couple and feed back on one another. Linear, siloed financial forecasts miss these coupled risks, so the claim capacity of money, which he calls transferable claims on the future, faces what he estimates (citing models) as about an 80% chance of collapse over 30 years. He asks wealth holders, especially sovereign funds and intergenerational family offices not bound by 'coded capital', to check the models and allocate even a small share to managing this existential risk to capital itself, through Dark Matter Labs' initiative Expanding Civilizational Optionality (xCO): options such as cooling Madrid, stabilizing aquifers, atmospheric river governance, zero-material urban growth, bioregional stabilization and the Arctic. Nate coins a 'civilizational Sharpe ratio' on air. They also discuss consciousness, doubt and humility, and courage as the invitation of the moment."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-09-23"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-10-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.IndyJohar ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/238-indy-johar> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/TGS-238-Indy-Johar-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CivilizationalOptionality, tgs:Concept.CoupledSystemicRisks, tgs:Concept.CivilizationalSharpeRatio,
        tgs:Concept.SpaceOfTheNecessary, tgs:Concept.ContinuityValueOfProvision, tgs:Concept.CodedCapital, tgs:Concept.FutureOfBeingHuman,
        tgs:Concept.MoneyAsClaimOnEnergy, tgs:Concept.HoloceneClimateStability, tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli, tgs:Concept.DividedBrain,
        tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.RobustnessVsPerformance ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_147_IndyJohar, tgs:Interview.TGS_230_OlivierHamant, tgs:Interview.TGS_95_SirDavidKing,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_85_WisdomNatureAndTheBrain, tgs:Interview.TGS_99_JeremyGrantham, tgs:Interview.TGS_155_JeremyGrantham,
        tgs:AnimatedVideo.TGS_182_TheSystemsScience, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_65_AndThenWhat,
        tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Work.CivilizationalOptionalityEssay, tgs:Work.MoneyAsAClaimOnFutureEnergy, tgs:Work.LoadingTheDiceAgainstPensions,
        tgs:Work.GlobalPolycrisisCausalMechanisms, tgs:Work.SeeingLikeAState, tgs:Organization.DarkMatterLabs ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate picks up where their last conversation ended: what does it mean to be human now? Johar describes a deep problem and a narrow one. The deep problem is our subject-object separation from the world (linear perspective, noun-oriented worldviews, reductionism), which let separation turn into dominion and into externality. The narrow problem is that AI is about to disrupt humans as units of labor and as units of cognition; married to an atomized, individuated self, he expects an economy that starts to 'expunge' people from itself, nations removing people to preserve their economics, and a pathway to war around 2028-2029, earlier than he once expected climate breakdown to bite. War-scale trauma, the falling unit cost of warfare and tools like CRISPR could make that position non-retrievable, so he calls for frontier labs on the future of being human (see tgs:Concept.FutureOfBeingHuman). Asked about a third role beyond labor and cognition, he points to consciousness and entanglement, citing Roger Penrose but leaving it open as conjecture. He reads the capital chasing AGI not as a bet on displacing labor but as a search for a higher-dimensional manifold in language models -- evocatively, 'touching a mirror of God' -- comparing it to the capital that flowed into alchemy and gave birth to chemistry; money is moving on a different theory of value. The 1968 Earthrise photo, after James Lovelock, was the planet becoming self-aware. On markets, Johar says twelve thousand years of civilization rested on Holocene predictability (see tgs:Concept.HoloceneClimateStability), and so did most financial accrual of wealth; we are now leaving zones of predictability in climate, demographics and energy supply, so 'all of our theories of alpha' may already be gone. Forecasts such as copper demand are linear and separated, while the systems are coupling and feeding back (see tgs:Concept.CoupledSystemicRisks); if allocators do not see this, he does not know how fiduciary duty is being fulfilled. Family offices have an advantage as unencumbered capital, unlike 'coded capital' locked into a remit years in advance (see tgs:Concept.CodedCapital). Nate cites Olivier Hamant's era of the standard deviation (TGS_230). Biophysical examples: Sir David King's case for stabilizing the Arctic, and the value of London's 3.8 million homes at stake from sea-level rise; and a bioregion in northern Spain where monoculture plantations dried the soil, mycelium died, hunting removed fauna, clouds pass over, rain runs off compacted ground and depopulation removes the people who would care, a regressive coupling into desertification and abandonment where land worth about 1,500 per hectare carries about 20,000 in fire and other liabilities. He expects a nutrition shock between late 2026 and 2027 from a super El Nino and the Strait of Hormuz crisis, with price whiplash driving inequality that makes the social contract fragile and 'weaponizable'. Playing devil's advocate (AI will solve this), Nate draws out Johar's reconstruction of Eric Schmidt's logic, a 'civilizational stack': whoever gets recursive intelligence gains the choice of freedom; valuing AGI on labor disruption leads to removing surplus people; compute drives an energy supercycle that could push energy costs toward zero and enable desalination, carbon sequestration and vertical farming, while its holder profits from volatility. Johar names the stack to show the scale of the play, not to endorse it: AI is a partial, rarefied mirror of consciousness, and Nate adds it leans on the left-hemisphere, narrow-boundary side of our phenotype (see tgs:Concept.DividedBrain). He rejects the meme that Earth can support only a billion people: the problem is the billion who live as he does. In the long arc, Earth spent its stored hydrocarbons to give birth to billions of human consciousnesses alongside machine and non-human consciousness, which is the real prize, not microchips or books; Nate asks whether climate change caused humans, and Johar calls this a Fermi paradox moment. Trained as an architect, he refuses to draw a single picture of the future, which would 'colonize' it; stepping stones include Whale Nation (a species nation, now an xCO project), Arctic stabilization as a new relational planetary governance, doubt-oriented rather than certainty-oriented machines, and a terra preta theory of land, cultivated as a multispecies open garden with micro-autonomous machines. Science, he says as a Sikh, is a socialized mechanism for holding uncertainty, not a provider of truths; he cites Descartes' 'I doubt, therefore I think, therefore I am', and argues that a civilization that cannot hold doubt cannot hold care. From 'quasi-certainties' -- we will have to stabilize the Arctic, cool Madrid by about 7.5 degrees -- he derives where capital should go: quasi-population-scale infrastructures and demand-side innovation, with the end goal of a system able to heal itself autopoietically. Nate's wide-boundary frame: about 100 trillion in GDP against roughly 400 trillion in debt and claims, which cannot all be spent on energy and materials; Johar names the coupled risks (nutrition, labor and cognitive collapse, geopolitical destabilization, climate volatility) and asks holders of wealth to understand the 'optionality collapse' facing their claims on the future -- not as a moral demand: 'I'm not inviting you to be kind. I'm just inviting you to be smart.' Nate coins a civilizational Sharpe ratio (see tgs:Concept.CivilizationalSharpeRatio) and cites Upton Sinclair. Dark Matter Labs' xCO routes aware capital to options that firebreak cascading risks: cooling a city, stabilizing an aquifer, atmospheric river governance, zero-material growth of a city, collective decision-making, beyond-labor theories of being human, and work in seven bioregions, about 17 options in all (see tgs:Concept.CivilizationalOptionality). Nate adds that those claims themselves have optionality and need not be spent on comfort, convenience and bling. On theory of change, Johar expects crises to be Overton window moments and wants to act fast enough to prevent large-scale violence; narratives spread like viruses, people feel before they find words, and he prefers courage to hope, which he finds too consumer-like. His pitch to wealth holders: check the models, which he says show about an 80% chance of collapse in money's claim capacity over 30 years; if they hold up, allocate some share, even 0.1-10%, to managing that risk, like fire insurance (Nate's analogy); pair existential-risk capital with participatory capital in new value chains, to recycle the roughly 400 trillion in claims. Terra preta and the Amazon as a cultivated open garden; James C. Scott's Seeing Like a State on simplification. Barriers: a kind, exhausted donor saying 'it's the best I can do', operating in the space of the possible rather than the necessary (see tgs:Concept.SpaceOfTheNecessary); the need for first-principles thinkers inoculated against the billion-person future and the bunker, willing to be agents in making the world; and 'good capital' so apologetic it seeks not to imprint on the world. Asked how he holds uncertainty (Nate gives himself a 25% haircut for being human), Johar discounts himself while noting his picture keeps sharpening, invites listeners to model it their own way, and says pooling capital is pooling claims and a life commitment, so xCO should be only a small part of anyone's portfolio. He redefines capital as transferable claims on the future, which civilizations sometimes pool intensely (Elon Musk's ventures, the electrification of New York), and portfolio as positions that expand optionality together rather than merely hedge; any limited partner should ask a fund for its worldview, since 'all the fundamentals are off': energy, demographics, M2 versus GDP, AI infrastructure, climate. Nate calls money our most intense supernormal stimulus (see tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli) and wonders how lockups would change decisions. Johar contrasts extracting a city's land-value uplift with investing in its buoyancy (a building's value comes from its placement, not its materials), and argues that the continuity of water, energy, nutrition, critical minerals and labor is massively underpriced (see tgs:Concept.ContinuityValueOfProvision), needing layered capital and derivative tools that price total asset losses, a response to the prisoner's dilemma Nate raises. He closes that we face 'a eye of a needle to an extraordinary future', with courage as the invitation; Nate mentions a lunch with Jeremy Grantham, who told him to use less inside-baseball language, and Johar says people will discover the words if they get the feeling."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "2026-10-15: built from the official transcript and show notes on the day after release. The episode title is not in the show notes extract; the prefLabel is composed from the show notes' description and should be replaced with the official title when confirmed. The canonical URL follows the site's {number}-{guest} slug pattern (147-indy-johar is confirmed in the show notes) and is not independently confirmed; no YouTube or Spotify link was available. Host introduction quoted from the auto-generated transcript with stutters removed ('civ- societies', 'e- an example'); the closing 'What a great dude. Please welcome Indy Johar' is omitted. Garbled words in the transcript: 'Olivia Hamant' is Olivier Hamant; 'Nia Trust and Hedda and Alexa on a whale' and 'Niatruss Wales nation' refer to Whale Nation, an xCO project in development; 'family officers' is family offices; 'Hilo community' is Hylo. Spoken figures recorded rather than corrected: Johar says four and a half meters of sea-level rise in Sir David King's scenario (the show notes cite King's ~7.5 m if Greenland's ice melts) and an average London home price of 668,000 pounds (show notes: ~550,000), giving 2.52 trillion; he says cooling Madrid by 'eight point five' and then 'seven point five' degrees (Dark Matter Labs' target is about 7.5 C); he gives world population as 7.5 and 8.5 billion (show notes: ~8.2 billion); Nate's 'a hundred trillion odd in GDP' and 'upwards of four hundred trillion in total debt' compare with the show notes' ~118 trillion and over 365 trillion. The 80% chance of collapse in money's claim capacity over 30 years is Johar's reading of unnamed models. Nate coins 'civilizational Sharpe ratio' on air. Not built: Expanding Civilizational Optionality (xCO), Whale Nation, Project 00 and the WikiHouse Foundation as separate entities (xCO is described under tgs:Concept.CivilizationalOptionality and tgs:Organization.DarkMatterLabs); Roger Penrose, James Lovelock, Eric Schmidt, Upton Sinclair, Rene Descartes and James C. Scott as Personas; the Platonic Representation Hypothesis, terra preta, Overton window and autopoiesis as Concepts. The Earthrise photo (1968) and Sir David King's Arctic refreezing case are mentioned but not linked to tgs:Concept.GlacierStabilization, which concerns glaciers specifically."@en ;
    .

tgs:Interview.TGS_239_RobinWallKimmerer a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-10-21"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-21"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Today I am joined by Robin Wall Kimmerer for a deep dive into the intersection of Western science and indigenous knowledge, especially through the lens of plants as teachers. Robin Wall Kimmerer is a plant ecologist, a bryologist, who is a botanist who works on mosses, liverworts, and hornworts. She is a writer and a member of the Citizen Potawatomi Nation. ... In this conversation, we discuss her journey of learning through plants, from the three sisters of corn, beans and squash, to mosses in boreal forests. She also shares some of the foundational Potawatomi worldview that shaped her research and scientific career. Most of all, Robin and I discuss how these ideas move beyond philosophy and actually influence how we live, or are going to live, and could even shape the systems around us if humanity chooses that path."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Robin Wall Kimmerer: \"Plants as Teachers, the Honorable Harvest and the Age of Remembering\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's first conversation with plant ecologist, bryologist and writer Robin Wall Kimmerer, an enrolled member of the Citizen Potawatomi Nation and author of Braiding Sweetgrass and The Serviceberry, recorded in person in New York City. Treating plants as teachers, she draws lessons from the Three Sisters polyculture (complementarity rather than competition) and from mosses (success as long-term persistence through smallness and cooperation), and braids Indigenous knowledge with Western science. She traces the polycrisis to the story of human exceptionalism and offers a kinship worldview in its place; explains the Honorable Harvest, a code of restraint for taking from relatives that Nate contrasts with our 'dishonorable harvest'; and shares the Potawatomi Seven Fires prophecy -- a fork between a scorched and a green path, reachable only by walking back to recover what was lost -- naming this moment the Age of Remembering. They discuss the grammar of animacy, the gift economy of The Serviceberry, restoration paired with re-story-ation, seven generations thinking and abundance as enoughness."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-09-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-10-21"^^xsd:date ;
