    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "OPENING: Nate calls her one of the most requested guests of recent years; they meet in person for the first time. PLANTS AS TEACHERS: as a Potawatomi woman and a Western-trained plant ecologist, Kimmerer treats plants as elders and teachers, invoking biomimicry (TGS_135). Her example is the Three Sisters polyculture of corn, beans and squash, which needs no tillage, pesticide or fertilizer because the beans fix nitrogen and the squash shades the soil -- a lesson in complementarity: 'we don't have to compete'. Nate asks whether we will need polycultures again at scale once fossil subsidies fade (he cites about 10 fossil calories per food calorie); she says yes, but the question is not how to scale up but how to 'scale it down to relationships with our home places'. TWO WAYS OF KNOWING: in the field she 'interviews' plants with experimental design and with intuition; Western science's claim to pure objectivity is a cultural construct, and Indigenous knowledge 'absolutely is science' (tgs:Concept.TwoWaysOfKnowing). MOSSES: thinking across scales through the fractal organization of nature; mosses in boreal forest and tundra hold more carbon than the trees, much of it as peat and permafrost; and mosses redefine success as long-term persistence through smallness and cooperation (tgs:Concept.SuccessAsLongTermPersistence), which Nate links to Olivier Hamant (TGS_230). THE GREAT GREEN WE: on cooperation (David Sloan Wilson, E. O. Wilson, multilevel selection), she asks what 'self' means in self-interest; the self has permeable boundaries reaching to the plants we eat and the forest we live in (tgs:Concept.KinshipWorldview). Elders are valued for continuity of knowledge. HUMAN EXCEPTIONALISM: the polycrisis is rooted in the story of what it means to be human -- Aristotle's ladder of nature, with plants at the bottom (tgs:Concept.HumanExceptionalism); her Haudenosaunee neighbors call humans the younger brothers of creation, which Nate says is simply science. LEARNING FROM INDIGENOUS KNOWLEDGE: beyond repair of past injustice, which must happen, her people hid and kept their knowledge because 'there would come a time when the whole world needed to remember'. She cites the 2019 IPBES assessment: biodiversity declines less on lands stewarded by Indigenous peoples; Nate asks whether low population density also explains this, and she agrees many factors contribute. THE HONORABLE HARVEST: a near-universal code of ethics for eating one's relatives -- ask permission, never take the first or more than half, share, be grateful, reciprocate, take only what is given (tgs:Concept.HonorableHarvest). Nate: our economy runs 'a dishonorable harvest at full speed'; wisdom as restraint (TGS_126); fossil energy spent as if interest rather than principal removed any need for restraint. 'Ethics don't come with a chromosome' -- they come from relationships and from learning from mistakes. THE SEVENTH FIRE: the Potawatomi Seven Fires prophecy, the fork between a burnt and a green path, walking back along the ancestor path to carry what was dropped through 'the bottleneck of the great narrowing', lighting the eighth fire (tgs:Concept.SevenFiresProphecy). Nate reads it as a species-wide rite of passage and Plotkin's ecological adulthood (TGS_146), and admits that when he began the podcast he thought Indigenous wisdom an irrelevant distraction: 'I now get it.' THE AGE OF REMEMBERING: after 500 years of dismembering, to re-member is to rejoin the family of life (tgs:Concept.AgeOfRemembering); whether in time she doesn't know, but she sees it rising at the grassroots, not among leaders. GOVERNANCE: she won't generalize across 574 nations, but women's leadership is often at the forefront, and seven generations thinking runs from great-grandparents to great-grandchildren (tgs:Concept.SevenGenerationsThinking); Indigenous communities are doing innovative climate-resilience work. Nate notes the coming El Niño (TGS_225). THE SERVICEBERRY: asked to write on economics, she wrote about a plant that passes on its abundance -- the gift economy versus the enclosure and manufactured scarcity of the market (tgs:Concept.GiftEconomy); Nate's distinction is that nature gives back. Humans can be medicine for the land; Nate calls humans a potential keystone species. RESTORATION AND RE-STORY-ATION: tallgrass prairie restoration as a success, done at scale; but restoring land without restoring our relationship to it is Sisyphean, and needs new stories (tgs:Concept.RestorationAndRestoryation), the work of her new nonprofit Plant Baby Plant. AI AND MARS: we have barely scratched the surface of natural intelligence; AI only with ethical guidelines, and the precautionary principle says don't; to those who want to go to Mars, 'please do'. GRAMMAR OF ANIMACY: Potawatomi verbs mark living beings as animate, while English calls every non-human being 'it' (tgs:Concept.GrammarOfAnimacy). Nate brings in Indy Johar's Earthrise as the Earth becoming self-aware (TGS_238); she asks whether we will finally understand why her people call the Earth Mother. ENOUGHNESS: abundance as enough, wealth as how much you give away, 'all flourishing is mutual'; be like the mosses, small and cooperative (tgs:Concept.Enoughness). CLOSING: the antidote to despair is to roll up your sleeves and work in solidarity with your community and reclaim agency; a student's answer at commencement -- this is the best possible time to be alive, because 'it matters where I stand'; she cares most, after family, for the community of life; with a magic wand she would plant forests -- 'plants know what to do about climate change'; she worries about defeatism and, rather than hope, harnesses love for the world."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-21 from the official transcript and show notes on the episode's release; guest bio captured in tgs:Human.RobinWallKimmerer. The show notes give no episode title, so the prefLabel is composed from the description and should be replaced with the official title when confirmed. The canonical URL follows the site's {number}-{guest} slug pattern and is unconfirmed. Dates: recorded 24 September 2026 per the show notes (consistent with Nate saying he recorded TGS_238 'yesterday'); dct:issued is 21 October 2026, the release date per MJSullivan; the 15 October 2026 on the show notes page is likely when the page was prepared. Transcript (auto-generated) corrections: 'Service Berry' is The Serviceberry; 'Plant Baby Plants' is Plant Baby Plant; 'dbaadendiziwin' (humility, one of the Seven Grandfather Teachings) left as transcribed; 'permeal' is permeable. Spoken details recorded rather than corrected, per the show notes: Kimmerer's 'around 80% of the world's remaining biodiversity is in Indigenous homelands' (the show notes find no basis for the 80% figure; Indigenous peoples manage or hold tenure over more than a quarter of the world's land); 'less than 1%' of the original tallgrass prairie remains (the show notes cite the National Park Service: less than 4% remains intact); '574 different Indigenous nations' (575 federally recognized tribes as of January 2026); Nate's 'ten to one' fossil inputs per food calorie (the show notes cite 13 to 1); the UN biodiversity report 'three years ago' is the 2019 IPBES Global Assessment. Kimmerer's Potawatomi sentences are not transcribed. Not built: David Pimentel (Nate's early influence, author of Food, Energy, and Society); Victoria Sweet, recorded the day before (episode not yet in the graph); Arne Naess and Enrique Salmon (cited in the show notes); Emergence Magazine; the Haudenosaunee Confederacy; the Three Sisters polyculture as a concept (cf. tgs:PracticeTerm.OneEarth_Polyculture) and Traditional Ecological Knowledge (tgs:PracticeTerm.TraditionalEcologicalKnowledge) as discusses targets, since thinkr:discusses points to concepts."@en ;
    .

